window.PYQ_DATA.add({"id":"px-ncert-math-complex","kind":"practice","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"title":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations · Practice","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","subject":"Maths","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","marking":{"mcq":[4,-1]},"rules":{},"note":"","questions":[{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-001","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":1,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"If $x + iy = \\frac{3+4i}{3-4i}$, where $x$ and $y$ are real, then the value of $x^2 + y^2$ is","image":"","options":["$25$","$1$","$\\frac{7}{25}$","$5$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $x+iy = \\frac{3+4i}{3-4i}$.\nFormula: $x^2+y^2 = |x+iy|^2$ and $\\left|\\frac{z_1}{z_2}\\right| = \\frac{|z_1|}{|z_2|}$.\nSubstituting: $|3+4i| = |3-4i| = 5$, so $|x+iy| = \\frac{5}{5} = 1$.\nSo $x^2+y^2 = 1^2 = 1$. The value $\\frac{7}{25}$ is only the real part $x$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-002","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":2,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: $\\sqrt{-4} \\times \\sqrt{-9} = 6$\nStatement II: The rule $\\sqrt{a} \\times \\sqrt{b} = \\sqrt{ab}$ does not hold when both $a$ and $b$ are negative real numbers.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"For $a > 0$, $\\sqrt{-a} = \\sqrt{a}\\,i$.\nSo $\\sqrt{-4} = 2i$ and $\\sqrt{-9} = 3i$.\nTheir product is $6i^2 = -6$, not 6. Statement I is false.\nThe value 6 comes only from wrongly writing $\\sqrt{(-4)(-9)} = \\sqrt{36}$.\nNCERT shows that $\\sqrt{a}\\sqrt{b} \\ne \\sqrt{ab}$ when both $a$ and $b$ are negative. Statement II is true.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-003","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":3,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"The value of $\\sqrt{-2}\\,\\sqrt{-8}+\\left(\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{-3}\\right)^2-\\left(\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{-3}\\right)^2$ is","image":"","options":["$-4+4\\sqrt{6}\\,i$","$4+4\\sqrt{6}\\,i$","$-4-4\\sqrt{6}\\,i$","$-4+2\\sqrt{6}\\,i$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"First term: $\\sqrt{-2}=\\sqrt2\\,i$ and $\\sqrt{-8}=2\\sqrt2\\,i$. Their product is $4i^2=-4$. Taking $+4$ via $\\sqrt{16}$ would be the error.\nSecond part: write $\\sqrt{-3}=\\sqrt3\\,i$ and use $z_1^2-z_2^2=(z_1+z_2)(z_1-z_2)$.\nHere $z_1+z_2=2\\sqrt2$ and $z_1-z_2=2\\sqrt3\\,i$.\nSo the difference of squares is $2\\sqrt2\\cdot2\\sqrt3\\,i=4\\sqrt6\\,i$.\nTotal: $-4+4\\sqrt6\\,i$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-004","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":4,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"The value of the product $i \\cdot i^2 \\cdot i^3 \\cdot i^4 \\cdots i^{100}$ is","image":"","options":["$1$","$-1$","$i$","$-i$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Multiplying powers of the same base adds the exponents, so the product is $i^{1+2+\\dots+100}$.\n$1 + 2 + \\dots + 100 = \\frac{100 \\times 101}{2} = 5050$.\nSince $5050 = 4(1262) + 2$, we get $i^{5050} = i^{4k+2} = -1$.\nThe tempting answer 1 comes from assuming that 100 factors must give $i^{4k}$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-005","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":5,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Let $\\alpha = 2 + i$, and let $\\beta$ be a complex number satisfying $|\\beta - \\alpha| = |1 - \\bar{\\alpha}\\beta|$. Then $|\\beta|$ equals","image":"","options":["$5$","$\\sqrt{5}$","$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}$","$1$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Square both sides using $|z|^2 = z\\bar{z}$.\nLeft side: $|\\beta-\\alpha|^2 = |\\beta|^2 + |\\alpha|^2 - 2\\,\\text{Re}(\\bar{\\alpha}\\beta)$.\nRight side: $|1-\\bar{\\alpha}\\beta|^2 = 1 + |\\alpha|^2|\\beta|^2 - 2\\,\\text{Re}(\\bar{\\alpha}\\beta)$.\nEquating and simplifying: $|\\beta|^2 + |\\alpha|^2 - 1 - |\\alpha|^2|\\beta|^2 = 0$, which factorises as $(1-|\\alpha|^2)(1-|\\beta|^2) = 0$.\nHere $|\\alpha|^2 = 5 \\neq 1$, so the first factor is non-zero. Hence $|\\beta|^2 = 1$, giving $|\\beta| = 1$.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-006","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":6,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Let $z_1 = a + ib$ and $z_2 = c + id$, where $a, b, c, d$ are real numbers. Which of the following is the correct condition for $z_1 = z_2$?","image":"","options":["$a + b = c + d$","$a = c$ and $b = d$","$a^2 + b^2 = c^2 + d^2$","$a = d$ and $b = c$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT defines two complex numbers $a + ib$ and $c + id$ to be equal if $a = c$ and $b = d$. In words, the real parts must match and the imaginary parts must match.\nOption (3) only says the moduli are equal. That is necessary for equality but not sufficient: $3 + 4i$ and $5$ have the same modulus but are different numbers.\nOption (1) mixes real and imaginary parts, and option (4) swaps them, so both are wrong.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-007","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":7,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"How many ordered pairs $(x, y)$ of real numbers satisfy $(x^2 - 5x + 6) + i(y^2 - 1) = 0$?","image":"","options":["0","1","2","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The right side is $0 = 0 + i0$, so both the real part and the imaginary part must be zero.\nReal part: $x^2 - 5x + 6 = 0$ factorises as $(x-2)(x-3) = 0$, so $x = 2$ or $x = 3$.\nImaginary part: $y^2 - 1 = 0$, so $y = 1$ or $y = -1$.\nEach $x$ can pair with each $y$, giving $2 \\times 2 = 4$ ordered pairs.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-008","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":8,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: $(3 + 2i)^2 - (3 - 2i)^2 = 24i$\nStatement II: The identity $z_1^2 - z_2^2 = (z_1 + z_2)(z_1 - z_2)$ holds for all complex numbers $z_1$ and $z_2$.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement II is true. NCERT lists $z_1^2 - z_2^2 = (z_1+z_2)(z_1-z_2)$ among identities valid for all complex numbers.\nStatement I: here $z_1 + z_2 = 6$ and $z_1 - z_2 = 4i$, so the expression is $6 \\times 4i = 24i$. True.\nDirect check: $(5 + 12i) - (5 - 12i) = 24i$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-009","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":9,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"The value of $(1 + i)^6$ is","image":"","options":["$-8i$","$8i$","$-8$","$8$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"First find the square: $(1+i)^2 = 1 + 2i + i^2 = 2i$.\nThen $(1+i)^6 = \\left[(1+i)^2\\right]^3 = (2i)^3 = 8i^3$.\nSince $i^3 = -i$, this gives $-8i$.\nOption (2) results from taking $i^3 = i$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-010","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":10,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements about the Argand plane:\n(i) The point representing $0 + 1i$ lies on the imaginary axis.\n(ii) The point representing $-2 + 3i$ lies in the second quadrant.\n(iii) $|-5 - 2i|$ equals the distance of the point $(-5, -2)$ from the origin, which is $\\sqrt{29}$.\n(iv) The point representing the conjugate of $1 - 2i$ is the reflection of $(1, -2)$ in the imaginary axis.\n(v) A complex number $z$ and its conjugate $\\bar{z}$ can be at different distances from the origin.\nHow many of the above statements are correct?","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: $0 + 1i$ is the point $(0, 1)$, which lies on the imaginary axis.\n(ii) Correct: $-2 + 3i$ is the point $(-2, 3)$, so x < 0 and y > 0, which is the second quadrant.\n(iii) Correct: the distance is $\\sqrt{(-5)^2 + (-2)^2} = \\sqrt{29}$.\n(iv) Incorrect: the conjugate $1 + 2i$ is the point $(1, 2)$, which is the reflection in the real axis, not the imaginary axis.\n(v) Incorrect: $|z| = |\\bar{z}| = \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2}$, so the two distances are always equal.\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-011","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":11,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"If $z_1 = 1 + 2i$ and $z_2 = 2 - i$, then $\\frac{z_1 + z_2 + 1}{z_1 - z_2 + 1}$ equals","image":"","options":["$\\frac{1}{3} + \\frac{4}{3}i$","$\\frac{1}{3} - \\frac{4}{3}i$","$-\\frac{1}{3} - \\frac{4}{3}i$","$\\frac{4}{3} - \\frac{1}{3}i$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Numerator: $z_1 + z_2 + 1 = (1 + 2 + 1) + i(2 - 1) = 4 + i$.\nDenominator: $z_1 - z_2 + 1 = (1 - 2 + 1) + i(2 + 1) = 3i$.\nMultiply top and bottom by $-i$: $\\frac{(4 + i)(-i)}{3i(-i)} = \\frac{-4i - i^2}{3} = \\frac{1 - 4i}{3}$.\nSo the value is $\\frac{1}{3} - \\frac{4}{3}i$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-012","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":12,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following complex numbers are represented by points lying on the circle of radius 5 centred at the origin of the Argand plane?\n$3 - 4i$, $-5 + 0i$, $1 + 2i$, $-4 - 3i$, $0 + 5i$, $2 + \\sqrt{21}\\,i$","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A point lies on this circle exactly when $|z| = \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2} = 5$.\nCheck each number:\n$|3 - 4i| = \\sqrt{9 + 16} = 5$ ✓\n$|-5| = 5$ ✓\n$|1 + 2i| = \\sqrt{5}$ ✗\n$|-4 - 3i| = 5$ ✓\n$|5i| = 5$ ✓\n$|2 + \\sqrt{21}i| = \\sqrt{4 + 21} = 5$ ✓\nSo 5 of the six numbers lie on the circle.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-013","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":13,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): $(1+i)^4 = -4$.\nReason (R): $(1+i)^2 = 2i$.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R: $(1+i)^2 = 1 + 2i + i^2 = 1 + 2i - 1 = 2i$, so R is true.\nA: $(1+i)^4 = [(1+i)^2]^2 = (2i)^2 = 4i^2 = -4$, so A is true.\nA is obtained directly by squaring the result in R, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-014","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":14,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following complex numbers are purely imaginary?\n(i) $\\frac{1 + i}{1 - i}$\n(ii) $\\frac{2 + 3i}{3 - 2i}$\n(iii) $\\frac{1 + 2i}{2 + i}$\n(iv) $\\frac{1}{-i}$\n(v) $\\frac{3 + i}{1 + 3i}$","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) $\\frac{(1 + i)^2}{2} = i$, so it is purely imaginary.\n(ii) $\\frac{(2 + 3i)(3 + 2i)}{13} = \\frac{13i}{13} = i$, so it is purely imaginary.\n(iii) $\\frac{(1 + 2i)(2 - i)}{5} = \\frac{4 + 3i}{5}$, so it is not.\n(iv) $\\frac{1}{-i} = \\frac{i}{-i \\cdot i} = i$, so it is purely imaginary.\n(v) $\\frac{(3 + i)(1 - 3i)}{10} = \\frac{6 - 8i}{10}$, so it is not.\nThat makes 3 purely imaginary numbers.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-015","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":15,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"The additive inverse of the complex number $-3 + 4i$ is","image":"","options":["$-3 - 4i$","$3 + 4i$","$3 - 4i$","$\\frac{-3-4i}{25}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The additive inverse of $z = a + ib$ is $-z = -a + i(-b)$, so that $z + (-z) = 0$.\nHere $a = -3$ and $b = 4$, so $-z = 3 - 4i$.\nCheck: $(-3 + 4i) + (3 - 4i) = 0 + i0$.\n$-3 - 4i$ is the conjugate, not the negative. $\\frac{-3-4i}{25}$ is the multiplicative inverse.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-016","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":16,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"If $x$ and $y$ are real numbers such that $(x+iy)(2-i)=5+5i$, then the value of $x^2+y^2$ is","image":"","options":["10","5","13","8"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Expand: $(x+iy)(2-i)=(2x+y)+i(2y-x)$.\nEquate real parts: $2x+y=5$. Equate imaginary parts: $2y-x=5$.\nFrom the second equation, $x=2y-5$. Substituting: $4y-10+y=5$, so $y=3$ and $x=1$.\nCheck: $(1+3i)(2-i)=2-i+6i+3=5+5i$.\nSo $x^2+y^2=1+9=10$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-017","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":17,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Let $S = \\sum_{k=1}^{10} \\left(k + i(-1)^k k\\right)$, i.e., $S = (1 - i) + (2 + 2i) + (3 - 3i) + \\dots + (10 + 10i)$. Then $S$ equals","image":"","options":["$55 - 5i$","$55 + 5i$","$55 + 55i$","$55 + 0i$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Addition is associative and commutative, so we can add real parts and imaginary parts separately.\nReal part: $1 + 2 + \\dots + 10 = 55$.\nImaginary part: $-1 + 2 - 3 + 4 - \\dots - 9 + 10$. Group as $(-1+2) + (-3+4) + \\dots + (-9+10)$, which is five pairs of 1, giving 5.\nSo $S = 55 + 5i$.\nThe option $55 - 5i$ comes from a sign slip in the pairing.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-018","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":18,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"For $z = 3 + 4i$, match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\n(A) $|z|$\n(B) Re $z$\n(C) Im $\\bar{z}$\n(D) $z\\bar{z}$\nList-II:\n(I) 5\n(II) 25\n(III) 3\n(IV) $-4$","image":"","options":["A-I, B-III, C-IV, D-II","A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II","A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) $|z| = \\sqrt{3^2 + 4^2} = 5$, so A matches I.\n(B) Re $z = 3$, so B matches III.\n(C) $\\bar{z} = 3 - 4i$, so Im $\\bar{z} = -4$ and C matches IV.\n(D) $z\\bar{z} = |z|^2 = 25$, so D matches II.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-019","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":19,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\n(P) $(1+i)^3 - (1-i)^3$\n(Q) $(1+i)^4$\n(R) $\\sqrt{-2} \\times \\sqrt{-18}$\n(S) $(\\sqrt{3} + i)^3$\nList-II:\n(1) $-6$\n(2) $8i$\n(3) $4i$\n(4) $-4$","image":"","options":["P-3, Q-4, R-1, S-2","P-2, Q-4, R-1, S-3","P-3, Q-1, R-4, S-2","P-4, Q-3, R-1, S-2"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(P) Use $(a+b)^3 - (a-b)^3 = 2(3a^2b + b^3)$ with $a = 1$, $b = i$: $2(3i - i) = 4i$, so P-3.\n(Q) $(1+i)^4 = (2i)^2 = -4$, so Q-4.\n(R) $(\\sqrt{2}i)(3\\sqrt{2}i) = 6i^2 = -6$, so R-1.\n(S) $(\\sqrt{3}+i)^3 = 3\\sqrt{3} + 9i + 3\\sqrt{3}i^2 + i^3 = 3\\sqrt{3} + 9i - 3\\sqrt{3} - i = 8i$, so S-2.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-020","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":20,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\n(P) $i^{-21}$\n(Q) $i^{-50}$\n(R) $i^{-100}$\n(S) $i^{-63}$\nList-II:\n(1) $1$\n(2) $-1$\n(3) $i$\n(4) $-i$","image":"","options":["P-4, Q-2, R-1, S-3","P-3, Q-2, R-1, S-4","P-4, Q-1, R-2, S-3","P-3, Q-1, R-2, S-4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Rewrite each exponent in the form $4k + r$ with $r \\in \\{0, 1, 2, 3\\}$.\nP: $-21 = 4(-6)+3$, so $i^{-21} = i^3 = -i$, which is (4).\nQ: $-50 = 4(-13)+2$, so $i^{-50} = -1$, which is (2).\nR: $-100 = 4(-25)$, so $i^{-100} = 1$, which is (1).\nS: $-63 = 4(-16)+1$, so $i^{-63} = i$, which is (3).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-021","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":21,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"For a real number $k$, the complex number $z = \\frac{k + 2i}{1 - 2i}$ is purely imaginary. Then $|z|$ equals","image":"","options":["$2$","$1$","$4$","$\\sqrt{5}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Multiply the numerator and the denominator by $1 + 2i$: $z = \\frac{(k + 2i)(1 + 2i)}{5} = \\frac{(k - 4) + i(2k + 2)}{5}$.\nFor $z$ to be purely imaginary, the real part must vanish: $k - 4 = 0$, so $k = 4$.\nThen $z = \\frac{10i}{5} = 2i$, which is non-zero, as required.\nSo $|z| = 2$.\nOption (3) gives the value of $k$, not $|z|$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-022","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":22,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"The value of $i^{10} + i^{11} + i^{12} + i^{13}$ is","image":"","options":["$0$","$1$","$-1$","$4$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Reduce each exponent modulo 4.\n$i^{10} = i^{4(2)+2} = -1$ and $i^{11} = i^{4(2)+3} = -i$.\n$i^{12} = i^{4(3)} = 1$ and $i^{13} = i^{4(3)+1} = i$.\nSum $= -1 - i + 1 + i = 0$.\nIn general, any four consecutive integral powers of $i$ add up to 0.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-023","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":23,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (mathematician) with List-II (contribution mentioned in the NCERT historical note on complex numbers).\nList-I:\n(a) Mahavira\n(b) Bhaskara\n(c) Euler\n(d) W. R. Hamilton\nList-II:\n(i) Regarded $a + ib$ as an ordered pair of real numbers $(a, b)$\n(ii) In 'Ganitasara Sangraha', said a negative quantity is not a square and so has no square root\n(iii) In 'Bijaganita' (1150), wrote that there is no square root of a negative quantity\n(iv) First introduced the symbol $i$ for $\\sqrt{-1}$","image":"","options":["(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Mahavira (850) first stated the difficulty clearly, in Ganitasara Sangraha → (ii).\n(b) Bhaskara wrote Bijaganita in 1150 → (iii).\n(c) Euler was the first to use the symbol $i$ for $\\sqrt{-1}$ → (iv).\n(d) Hamilton (about 1830) treated $a+ib$ as the ordered pair $(a,b)$ → (i).\nThe tempting swaps (Mahavira with Bhaskara, or Euler with Hamilton) do not match the note.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-024","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":24,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are equal to 1?\n$i^{-4}$, $i^{2024}$, $(-i)^{8}$, $i^{-6}$, $\\left(\\frac{1}{i}\\right)^{12}$, $i^{-38}$","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"$i^{-4} = \\frac{1}{i^4} = 1$. Equals 1.\n$i^{2024}$: $2024 = 4(506)$, so it equals 1.\n$(-i)^8 = (-1)^8 i^8 = 1$. Equals 1.\n$i^{-6}$: $-6 = 4(-2)+2$, so it equals $-1$.\n$\\left(\\frac{1}{i}\\right)^{12} = (-i)^{12} = 1$. Equals 1.\n$i^{-38}$: $-38 = 4(-10)+2$, so it equals $-1$.\nFour of the six expressions equal 1.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-025","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":25,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are real numbers?\n(i) $i^{102}$\n(ii) $i^{-39}$\n(iii) $(1+i)^4$\n(iv) $i^{13}+i^{15}$\n(v) $\\frac{1+i}{1-i}$\n(vi) $z\\bar{z}$, where $z=2-3i$","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) $102=4(25)+2$, so $i^{102}=-1$. Real.\n(ii) $i^{39}=-i$, so $i^{-39}=\\frac{1}{-i}=i$. Not real.\n(iii) $(1+i)^2=2i$, so $(1+i)^4=(2i)^2=-4$. Real.\n(iv) $i^{13}+i^{15}=i+(-i)=0$. Real.\n(v) $\\frac{(1+i)^2}{(1-i)(1+i)}=\\frac{2i}{2}=i$. Not real.\n(vi) $z\\bar{z}=|z|^2=13$. Real.\nSo four of them are real.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-026","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":26,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Let $k_1$ be the real value of $k$ for which $\\frac{1 + 2i}{2 + ki}$ is purely imaginary, and $k_2$ the real value of $k$ for which it is purely real. Then $k_1 + k_2$ equals","image":"","options":["5","3","-3","-5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Rationalise: $\\frac{(1+2i)(2-ki)}{4+k^2}$.\nThe numerator expands to $2 - ki + 4i - 2ki^2 = (2 + 2k) + i(4 - k)$.\nPurely imaginary: $2 + 2k = 0$, so $k_1 = -1$. The imaginary part is then $5 \\neq 0$, so this is valid.\nPurely real: $4 - k = 0$, so $k_2 = 4$.\n$k_1 + k_2 = -1 + 4 = 3$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-027","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":27,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In the Argand plane, the point representing $2 + 0i$ lies on the real axis.\nStatement II: Every point on the imaginary axis represents a complex number of the form $a + i0$.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is true. $2 + 0i$ is the point $(2, 0)$, which lies on the x-axis, i.e., the real axis.\nStatement II is false. Points on the imaginary axis have the form $(0, b)$, so they represent $0 + ib$.\nNumbers of the form $a + i0$ lie on the real axis instead.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-028","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":28,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The numbers $5+\\sqrt{-15}$ and $5-\\sqrt{-15}$, which Cardan obtained, satisfy both $x+y=10$ and $xy=40$.\nStatement II: Cardan accepted these numbers as meaningful solutions and used them to show that a polynomial equation has as many roots as its degree.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I: $\\sqrt{-15} = i\\sqrt{15}$. The sum is $(5+i\\sqrt{15}) + (5-i\\sqrt{15}) = 10$. The product is $25 - i^2(15) = 25+15 = 40$. So Statement I is true.\nStatement II: Cardan discarded these numbers as 'useless'. It was Albert Girard (about 1625) who accepted them and linked them to the number of roots equalling the degree. So Statement II is false.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-029","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":29,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["For every non-zero complex number $z$, $z^{-1} = \\frac{\\bar{z}}{|z|^2}$","The complex number $0 + i0$ has no multiplicative inverse","The multiplicative inverse of $i$ is $-i$","The multiplicative inverse of $1 + i$ is $1 - i$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: this follows from $z\\bar{z} = |z|^2$.\n(2) Correct: $0 \\cdot w = 0 \\neq 1$ for every $w$, so 0 has no inverse.\n(3) Correct: $i \\cdot (-i) = -i^2 = 1$.\n(4) Incorrect: $(1 + i)(1 - i) = 2$, not 1. The true inverse is $\\frac{1 - i}{2}$.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-030","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":30,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"If $3x + i(2x + y) = 6 - 2i$, where $x, y \\in \\mathbb{R}$, then $y$ equals:","image":"","options":["$-2$","$2$","$-6$","$-8$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $3x + i(2x + y) = 6 - 2i$ with $x, y$ real.\nReal parts: $3x = 6$, so $x = 2$.\nImaginary parts: $2x + y = -2$.\nSubstituting $x = 2$: $4 + y = -2$, so $y = -6$.\nOption (1) comes from wrongly taking $y = -2$ directly, without subtracting $2x$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-031","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":31,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"The value of $(4 - 2i) - (-1 + 5i)$ is","image":"","options":["$5 - 7i$","$3 + 3i$","$5 + 3i$","$3 - 7i$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"By definition, $z_1 - z_2 = z_1 + (-z_2)$.\n$-(-1 + 5i) = 1 - 5i$.\nSo $(4 - 2i) + (1 - 5i) = (4 + 1) + i(-2 - 5) = 5 - 7i$.\nThe option $3 + 3i$ comes from adding $z_2$ instead of its negative.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-032","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":32,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is equal to $-1$?","image":"","options":["$i^{102}$","$i^{103}$","$i^{100}$","$i^{-101}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(1) $102 = 4(25)+2$, so $i^{102} = i^2 = -1$. This option is correct.\n(2) $103 = 4(25)+3$, so $i^{103} = -i$.\n(3) $100 = 4(25)$, so $i^{100} = 1$.\n(4) $-101 = 4(-26)+3$, so $i^{-101} = i^3 = -i$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-033","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":33,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: If $a + ib = c + id$, where $a, b, c, d$ are real, then $a^2 + b^2 = c^2 + d^2$.\nStatement II: If two complex numbers have equal moduli, then the two complex numbers are equal.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I: equality gives $a = c$ and $b = d$. Squaring and adding, $a^2 + b^2 = c^2 + d^2$, so it is true.\nStatement II: take $3 + 4i$ and $5 + 0i$. Both have modulus 5.\nBut their imaginary parts are 4 and 0, which differ, so the numbers are not equal. Hence it is false.\nEquality implies equal moduli, but equal moduli do not imply equality.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-034","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":34,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"The real numbers $x$ and $y$ satisfy the condition that $(x + iy)(1 + 2i)$ is the conjugate of $5 - 5i$. The ordered pair $(x, y)$ is","image":"","options":["$(3, 1)$","$(3, -1)$","$(-1, 3)$","$(1, 3)$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The conjugate of $5 - 5i$ is $5 + 5i$.\nExpand the product, using $i^2 = -1$: $(x + iy)(1 + 2i) = x + 2xi + iy + 2i^2y = (x - 2y) + i(2x + y)$.\nEquate real parts: $x - 2y = 5$. Equate imaginary parts: $2x + y = 5$.\nFrom $y = 5 - 2x$: $x - 2(5 - 2x) = 5$, so $5x = 15$. This gives $x = 3$ and $y = -1$.\nWhy $(3, 1)$ fails: $(3 + i)(1 + 2i) = 1 + 7i$, which is not $5 + 5i$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-035","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":35,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: $\\sqrt{-4}\\times\\sqrt{-9}=-6$.\nStatement II: $\\sqrt{a}\\times\\sqrt{b}=\\sqrt{ab}$ holds even when both $a$ and $b$ are negative real numbers.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I: $\\sqrt{-4}=2i$ and $\\sqrt{-9}=3i$. Their product is $6i^2=-6$, so it is true.\nStatement II: if the rule held, we would get $\\sqrt{(-4)(-9)}=\\sqrt{36}=6$, which contradicts Statement I. NCERT shows that $\\sqrt{a}\\sqrt{b}\\neq\\sqrt{ab}$ when both $a$ and $b$ are negative. So it is false.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-036","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":36,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Let $z = \\frac{(3-2i)(2+3i)}{(1+2i)(2-i)}$.\nStatement I: $|z| = \\frac{13}{5}$.\nStatement II: $\\bar{z} = \\frac{63}{25} - \\frac{16}{25}i$.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I: $|z| = \\frac{|3-2i||2+3i|}{|1+2i||2-i|} = \\frac{\\sqrt{13} \\cdot \\sqrt{13}}{\\sqrt{5} \\cdot \\sqrt{5}} = \\frac{13}{5}$. Correct.\nStatement II: The numerator is $12+5i$ and the denominator is $4+3i$.\nSo $z = \\frac{(12+5i)(4-3i)}{25} = \\frac{63-16i}{25}$.\nHence $\\bar{z} = \\frac{63}{25} + \\frac{16}{25}i$, so Statement II gives $z$ itself, not $\\bar{z}$. Incorrect.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-037","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":37,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Every real number $a$ can be regarded as a complex number.\nStatement II: For $z = 3 - 4i$, Im $z = -4i$.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is true. Any real number $a$ can be written as $a + i\\,0$, which has the form $a + ib$ with $b = 0$.\nStatement II is false. In $z = a + ib$, the imaginary part is the real number $b$. For $3 - 4i$, Im $z = -4$, not $-4i$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-038","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":38,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Let $k$ be a real number and $z = (k^2 - 3k + 2) + i(k^2 - 4)$. The value(s) of $k$ for which $z$ is a non-zero purely imaginary number is/are:","image":"","options":["$k = 2$ only","$k = 1$ only","$k = 1$ or $k = 2$","$k = -2$ only"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"For $z$ to be purely imaginary, we need Re $z = 0$.\nSo $k^2 - 3k + 2 = 0$, which factors as $(k - 1)(k - 2) = 0$, giving $k = 1$ or $k = 2$.\nFor $z$ to be non-zero, we also need Im $z = k^2 - 4 \\neq 0$, so $k \\neq \\pm 2$.\nThis rejects $k = 2$, which would give $z = 0$.\nAt $k = 1$: $z = -3i$, a non-zero purely imaginary number. So only $k = 1$ works.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-039","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":39,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"For complex numbers $z_1$ and $z_2$, which of the following statements is NOT always true?","image":"","options":["$\\overline{z_1+z_2} = \\bar z_1 + \\bar z_2$","$|z_1+z_2| = |z_1|+|z_2|$","$z_1\\bar z_1 = |z_1|^2$","$|\\bar z_1| = |z_1|$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) True: the conjugate of a sum is the sum of the conjugates.\n(2) Not always true: take $z_1 = 1$ and $z_2 = -1$. Then $|z_1+z_2| = 0$, but $|z_1|+|z_2| = 2$.\n(3) True: $(a+ib)(a-ib) = a^2+b^2$.\n(4) True: $a+ib$ and $a-ib$ both have modulus $\\sqrt{a^2+b^2}$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-040","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":40,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The multiplicative inverse of $3+4i$ is $\\frac{3-4i}{5}$.\nStatement II: For a non-zero complex number $z$, $z^{-1} = \\frac{\\bar{z}}{|z|^2}$.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement II is correct. Since $z\\bar{z} = |z|^2$, dividing both sides by $z|z|^2$ gives $z^{-1} = \\frac{\\bar{z}}{|z|^2}$.\nFor $z = 3+4i$: $|z|^2 = 9+16 = 25$.\nSo $z^{-1} = \\frac{3-4i}{25}$, not $\\frac{3-4i}{5}$. Statement I divides by $|z|$ instead of $|z|^2$, so it is incorrect.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-041","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":41,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"The conjugate of $\\frac{(2+i)(1-3i)}{1+i}$ is","image":"","options":["$5i$","$-5i$","$5$","$5+5i$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Numerator: $(2+i)(1-3i) = 2-6i+i-3i^2 = 5-5i$.\nRationalise: $\\frac{5-5i}{1+i} \\times \\frac{1-i}{1-i} = \\frac{5-5i-5i+5i^2}{2} = \\frac{-10i}{2} = -5i$.\nSo the expression equals $-5i$, and its conjugate is $5i$.\nOption (2) is the value of the expression itself, not its conjugate.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-042","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":42,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): If $x$ and $y$ are real and $(x+2y)+i(2x-y)=5+5i$, then $x=1$ and $y=2$.\nReason (R): Two complex numbers $a+ib$ and $c+id$ are equal if and only if $a=c$ and $b=d$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"R is the NCERT definition of equality of complex numbers, so R is true.\nUsing R, equate parts: $x+2y=5$ and $2x-y=5$.\nFrom the second equation, $y=2x-5$. Substituting gives $x+4x-10=5$, so $x=3$ and $y=1$.\nCheck the claimed values $x=1, y=2$: $2x-y=0\\neq5$. So A is false.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-043","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":43,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"The complex number $z = -2 + 3i$ is represented by point B in the Argand plane. Which point represents $\\bar{z}$?","image":"","options":["$(2, 3)$","$(-2, -3)$","$(2, -3)$","$(3, -2)$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The conjugate of $z = -2 + 3i$ is $\\bar{z} = -2 - 3i$.\n$\\bar{z}$ corresponds to the point $(-2, -3)$.\nGeometrically, $(x, -y)$ is the mirror image of $(x, y)$ in the real axis.\nOption (1), $(2, 3)$, is the mirror image in the imaginary axis. That point represents $-\\bar{z}$, not $\\bar{z}$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-044","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":44,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are purely real numbers?\n(a) $(1+i)^2$\n(b) $(1+i)^4$\n(c) $z + \\bar{z}$, where $z = 2 + 3i$\n(d) $\\frac{1+i}{1-i}$\n(e) $i^{-35}$\n(f) $(2+3i)(2-3i)$","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(a) $(1+i)^2 = 1 + 2i - 1 = 2i$. Not real.\n(b) $(1+i)^4 = (2i)^2 = -4$. Real.\n(c) $z + \\bar{z} = 2 \\times 2 = 4$. Real.\n(d) $\\frac{(1+i)^2}{(1-i)(1+i)} = \\frac{2i}{2} = i$. Not real.\n(e) $i^{-35} = i^{-36} \\cdot i = i$. Not real.\n(f) $4 + 9 = 13$. Real.\nSo 3 of them are real.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-045","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":45,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are purely real numbers? ($z$ denotes any complex number.)\n(i) $(1+i)^4$\n(ii) $(1+i)^3$\n(iii) $(2+3i)(2-3i)$\n(iv) $\\frac{1+i}{1-i}$\n(v) $\\frac{z-\\bar{z}}{i}$","image":"","options":["1","2","4","3"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(i) $(1+i)^2=2i$, so $(1+i)^4=(2i)^2=-4$. Real ✔\n(ii) $(1+i)^3=2i(1+i)=-2+2i$. Not real ✘\n(iii) $(2+3i)(2-3i)=4+9=13$. Real ✔\n(iv) $\\frac{(1+i)^2}{(1-i)(1+i)}=\\frac{2i}{2}=i$. Not real ✘\n(v) For $z=x+iy$, $z-\\bar z=2iy$, so $\\frac{2iy}{i}=2y$. Real ✔\nThree of them are purely real.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-046","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":46,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"The complex numbers $z_1 = 2 + 4i$ and $z_2 = -5 - 2i$ are plotted in the Argand plane as points A and E. What is the distance AE?","image":"","options":["$\\sqrt{85}$","$\\sqrt{13}$","$\\sqrt{37}$","$85$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: A is $(2, 4)$ and E is $(-5, -2)$.\nFormula: $AE = |z_1 - z_2| = \\sqrt{(x_1 - x_2)^2 + (y_1 - y_2)^2}$.\nSubstituting: $z_1 - z_2 = 7 + 6i$, so $AE = \\sqrt{7^2 + 6^2} = \\sqrt{49 + 36}$.\nSo $AE = \\sqrt{85} \\approx 9.22$.\nOption (2), $\\sqrt{13}$, comes from $|z_1 + z_2| = |-3 + 2i|$, i.e., adding instead of subtracting.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-047","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":47,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"For non-zero complex numbers $z_1$ and $z_2$, which of the following statements is INCORRECT in general?","image":"","options":["$\\overline{z_1 z_2} = \\bar{z}_1 \\, \\bar{z}_2$","$|z_1 + z_2| = |z_1| + |z_2|$","$\\left|\\frac{z_1}{z_2}\\right| = \\frac{|z_1|}{|z_2|}$","$z_1 \\bar{z}_1 = |z_1|^2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Option (1) is a standard NCERT result, so it is correct.\nOption (3) holds because modulus is multiplicative, so it is correct.\nOption (4) holds because $(a+ib)(a-ib) = a^2 + b^2$, so it is correct.\nOption (2) is false in general. Take $z_1 = 1$ and $z_2 = -1$: then $|z_1 + z_2| = 0$, but $|z_1| + |z_2| = 2$.\nModulus is multiplicative, but it is not additive.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-048","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":48,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"If $(x+iy)^3 = u + iv$, where $x, y, u, v$ are real and $xy \\neq 0$, then $\\frac{u}{x} - \\frac{v}{y}$ equals","image":"","options":["$4(x^2 - y^2)$","$2(x^2 + y^2)$","$-2(x^2 + y^2)$","$-4(x^2 + y^2)$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Expanding: $(x+iy)^3 = x^3 + 3x^2(iy) + 3x(iy)^2 + (iy)^3 = (x^3 - 3xy^2) + i(3x^2y - y^3)$.\nSo $u = x^3-3xy^2$ and $v = 3x^2y - y^3$.\nThen $\\frac{u}{x} = x^2 - 3y^2$ and $\\frac{v}{y} = 3x^2 - y^2$.\nSubtracting: $\\frac{u}{x} - \\frac{v}{y} = -2x^2 - 2y^2 = -2(x^2+y^2)$.\nOption (1) is the sum $\\frac{u}{x} + \\frac{v}{y}$, not the difference.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-049","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":49,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"If $x$ and $y$ are real numbers such that $(2x - y) + i(x + 3y) = 7 + 7i$, then the value of $x + y$ is","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Two complex numbers are equal only when their real parts are equal and their imaginary parts are equal.\nReal parts: $2x - y = 7$. Imaginary parts: $x + 3y = 7$.\nFrom the second equation, $x = 7 - 3y$. Substituting into the first: $2(7 - 3y) - y = 7$, so $14 - 7y = 7$ and $y = 1$.\nThen $x = 7 - 3 = 4$.\nCheck: $(8 - 1) + i(4 + 3) = 7 + 7i$. So $x + y = 5$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-050","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":50,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Expressed in the form $a+ib$, $(2+3i)^3$ equals","image":"","options":["$-46+9i$","$-46-9i$","$62+9i$","$-46+63i$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Use $(z_1+z_2)^3=z_1^3+3z_1^2z_2+3z_1z_2^2+z_2^3$ with $z_1=2$, $z_2=3i$.\nTerms: $2^3=8$; $3(4)(3i)=36i$; $3(2)(3i)^2=6(-9)=-54$; $(3i)^3=27i^3=-27i$.\nSum: $(8-54)+(36-27)i=-46+9i$.\nOption (4) comes from taking $i^3=+i$, which is wrong because $i^3=-i$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-051","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":51,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Match the complex numbers in List-I with their positions in the Argand plane in List-II.\nList-I:\n(A) $2 + 4i$\n(B) $-2 + 3i$\n(C) $1 - 2i$\n(D) $0 + 1i$\nList-II:\n(I) Fourth quadrant\n(II) Positive imaginary axis\n(III) First quadrant\n(IV) Second quadrant","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) $2 + 4i$ is the point $(2, 4)$. Here $x > 0, y > 0$, so it is in the first quadrant: III.\n(B) $-2 + 3i$ is the point $(-2, 3)$. Here $x < 0, y > 0$, so it is in the second quadrant: IV.\n(C) $1 - 2i$ is the point $(1, -2)$. Here $x > 0, y < 0$, so it is in the fourth quadrant: I.\n(D) $0 + 1i$ is the point $(0, 1)$, which lies on the positive imaginary axis: II.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-052","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":52,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"If $(2 + i)^3 = u + iv$, where $u, v$ are real, then the value of $\\frac{u}{2} + \\frac{v}{1}$ is","image":"","options":["$12$","$20$","$6$","$-12$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $x = 2$, $y = 1$, $(x+iy)^3 = u+iv$.\nExpand: $(2+i)^3 = 8 + 3(4)(i) + 3(2)(i^2) + i^3 = 8 + 12i - 6 - i = 2 + 11i$.\nSo $u = 2$ and $v = 11$, giving $\\frac{u}{2} + \\frac{v}{1} = 1 + 11 = 12$.\nCheck with the identity $\\frac{u}{x}+\\frac{v}{y} = 4(x^2-y^2)$: $4(4-1) = 12$. ✓\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-053","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":53,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"In the Argand plane, the complex number $z$ is represented by the point P$(-3, 4)$. Let Q be the point representing $\\bar{z}$ and let O be the origin. Which of the following is correct?","image":"","options":["Q is $(-3,-4)$ and OQ $=5$","Q is $(3,4)$ and OQ $=5$","Q is $(3,-4)$ and OQ $=5$","Q is $(-3,-4)$ and OQ $=7$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Here $z=-3+4i$, so $\\bar{z}=-3-4i$, which is the point Q$(-3,-4)$.\nGeometrically, Q is the mirror image of P in the real axis.\nOQ $=|\\bar{z}|=\\sqrt{(-3)^2+(-4)^2}=5$.\n$(3,4)$ represents $-\\bar{z}$ (the reflection in the imaginary axis), and $(3,-4)$ represents $-z$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-054","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":54,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): For every complex number $z$, the points representing $z$ and $\\bar{z}$ in the Argand plane are at the same distance from the origin.\nReason (R): The point representing $\\bar{z}$ is the mirror image of the point representing $z$ in the real axis.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true. If $z = x + iy$ is the point $(x, y)$, then $\\bar{z}$ is $(x, -y)$, its reflection in the real axis.\nThe origin lies on the real axis, and a reflection preserves distances. So both points are equally far from O.\nThis also checks out algebraically: $|z| = |\\bar{z}| = \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2}$.\nSo A is true, and R explains it.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-055","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":55,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Let $(x+iy)^3 = u + iv$, where $x = 2$ and $y = 1$. The value of $\\frac{u}{x} + \\frac{v}{y}$ is","image":"","options":["$6$","$16$","$10$","$12$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: $x=2$, $y=1$.\nExpanding: $(2+i)^3 = 8 + 3(4)(i) + 3(2)(i^2) + i^3 = 8 + 12i - 6 - i = 2 + 11i$.\nSo $u = 2$ and $v = 11$.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{u}{x} + \\frac{v}{y} = \\frac{2}{2} + \\frac{11}{1} = 12$.\nCheck: the general result $4(x^2-y^2) = 4(4-1) = 12$ agrees.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-056","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":56,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: $(1+i)^6=-8i$.\nStatement II: $(1+i)^2=2i$.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement II: $(1+i)^2=1+2i+i^2=1+2i-1=2i$. True.\nStatement I: $(1+i)^6=\\left[(1+i)^2\\right]^3=(2i)^3=8i^3=8(-i)=-8i$. True.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-057","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":57,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"The value of $i^{25} + i^{50}$ is","image":"","options":["$-1 + i$","$1 + i$","$-1 - i$","$1 - i$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"$25 = 4(6)+1$, so $i^{25} = i$.\n$50 = 4(12)+2$, so $i^{50} = -1$.\nAdding gives $i + (-1) = -1 + i$.\nOption (2) is the tempting wrong choice: it wrongly takes $i^{50} = +1$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-058","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":58,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"The set of all real values of $x$ satisfying $|1-i|^x = 2^x$ is","image":"","options":["$\\{0\\}$","the empty set","the set of all real numbers","$\\{0, 2\\}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Step 1: $|1-i| = \\sqrt{1^2+(-1)^2} = \\sqrt{2}$.\nStep 2: The equation becomes $(\\sqrt{2})^x = 2^x$, that is, $2^{x/2} = 2^x$.\nStep 3: Equating the exponents gives $\\frac{x}{2} = x$, so $x = 0$.\nStep 4: There is no non-zero solution, so the set is $\\{0\\}$. It is not empty, because $x=0$ satisfies $1=1$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-059","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":59,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"The multiplicative inverse of $3+4i$ is","image":"","options":["$\\frac{3}{25}-\\frac{4}{25}i$","$\\frac{3}{5}-\\frac{4}{5}i$","$-\\frac{3}{25}+\\frac{4}{25}i$","$\\frac{3}{25}+\\frac{4}{25}i$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Formula: $z^{-1}=\\frac{a}{a^2+b^2}+i\\frac{-b}{a^2+b^2}$.\nHere $a=3,\\ b=4$, so $a^2+b^2=9+16=25$.\nThis gives $z^{-1}=\\frac{3}{25}-\\frac{4}{25}i$.\nOption (2) divides by $|z|=5$ instead of $|z|^2=25$.\nCheck: $(3+4i)(3-4i)/25=25/25=1$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-060","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":60,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\n(A) $(1+i)^8$\n(B) $(1-i)^6$\n(C) $\\left(\\frac{1+i}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right)^{6}$\n(D) $i^{2026}$\nList-II:\n(I) $8i$\n(II) $-i$\n(III) 16\n(IV) $-1$","image":"","options":["A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV","A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV","A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key results: $(1+i)^2 = 2i$ and $(1-i)^2 = -2i$.\n(A) $(1+i)^8 = (2i)^4 = 16i^4 = 16$, so A matches III.\n(B) $(1-i)^6 = (-2i)^3 = -8i^3 = 8i$, so B matches I.\n(C) $\\left(\\frac{(1+i)^2}{2}\\right)^3 = i^3 = -i$, so C matches II.\n(D) $2026 = 4 \\times 506 + 2$, so $i^{2026} = -1$ and D matches IV.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-061","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":61,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(a) $\\frac{1}{3+4i}$\n(b) Conjugate of $(2+i)^2$\n(c) $\\frac{5}{3+4i}$\n(d) $i(3+4i)$\nList-II\n(p) $3-4i$\n(q) $\\frac{3-4i}{25}$\n(r) $-4+3i$\n(s) $\\frac{3-4i}{5}$","image":"","options":["a-q, b-p, c-s, d-r","a-s, b-p, c-q, d-r","a-q, b-r, c-s, d-p","a-p, b-q, c-r, d-s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) $\\frac{1}{3+4i}=\\frac{\\bar{z}}{|z|^2}=\\frac{3-4i}{25}$, which matches (q).\n(b) $(2+i)^2=4+4i-1=3+4i$, and its conjugate is $3-4i$, which matches (p).\n(c) $\\frac{5(3-4i)}{25}=\\frac{3-4i}{5}$, which matches (s).\n(d) $3i+4i^2=-4+3i$, which matches (r).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-062","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":62,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"If $z_1 = 4 + 2i$ and $z_2 = 1 + i$, then $\\frac{z_1}{z_2}$ in the form $a + ib$ is","image":"","options":["$3 + i$","$3 - i$","$1 - 3i$","$4 + 2i$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Multiply the numerator and the denominator by $\\bar{z_2} = 1 - i$.\nNumerator: $(4 + 2i)(1 - i) = 4 - 4i + 2i - 2i^2 = 6 - 2i$.\nDenominator: $(1 + i)(1 - i) = 1 + 1 = 2$.\nSo $\\frac{z_1}{z_2} = \\frac{6 - 2i}{2} = 3 - i$.\nOption (1) comes from a sign error in the imaginary part.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-063","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":63,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Which complex number is the multiplicative identity in the set of complex numbers? It is the number $e$ with $z\\cdot e=z$ for every complex number $z$.","image":"","options":["$0+i0$","$1+i0$","$0+i1$","$1+i1$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that $1+i0$ (written as 1) is the multiplicative identity.\nCheck: $(a+ib)(1+i0)=(a\\cdot1-b\\cdot0)+i(a\\cdot0+b\\cdot1)=a+ib$.\n$0+i0$ is the additive identity, not the multiplicative one.\n$0+i1=i$ fails, because $(a+ib)\\,i=-b+ia$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-064","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":64,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Let $z_1 = 1 + 2i$ and $z_2 = 3 - i$. Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\n(A) $z_1 + z_2$\n(B) $z_1 - z_2$\n(C) $z_2 - z_1$\n(D) $z_1 + (-z_1)$\nList-II:\n(I) $2 - 3i$\n(II) $0 + i0$\n(III) $4 + i$\n(IV) $-2 + 3i$","image":"","options":["A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(A) $(1+3) + i(2-1) = 4 + i$, which matches (III).\n(B) $(1-3) + i(2-(-1)) = -2 + 3i$, which matches (IV).\n(C) $z_2 - z_1 = -(z_1 - z_2) = 2 - 3i$, which matches (I).\n(D) A number plus its additive inverse gives $0 + i0$, which matches (II).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-065","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":65,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): $\\frac{1 + i}{1 - i} = i$.\nReason (R): Multiplying the numerator and the denominator of $\\frac{z_1}{z_2}$ by $\\bar{z_2}$ makes the denominator the real number $|z_2|^2$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true: $z_2\\bar{z_2} = |z_2|^2$, which is a real number.\nA is true. Multiply top and bottom by $1 + i$: $\\frac{(1 + i)^2}{(1 - i)(1 + i)} = \\frac{2i}{2} = i$.\nA is obtained exactly by the method stated in R, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-066","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":66,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"The modulus of $\\frac{(1+2i)^3 (3-i)^2}{(2+i)^4}$ is","image":"","options":["$2\\sqrt{5}$","$4\\sqrt{5}$","$\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}$","10"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Formula: $|z_1 z_2| = |z_1||z_2|$ and $|z_1/z_2| = |z_1|/|z_2|$, so $|z^n| = |z|^n$.\nModuli: $|1+2i| = \\sqrt{5}$, $|3-i| = \\sqrt{10}$, $|2+i| = \\sqrt{5}$.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{(\\sqrt{5})^3 (\\sqrt{10})^2}{(\\sqrt{5})^4} = \\frac{5\\sqrt{5} \\times 10}{25}$.\nSo the modulus $= 2\\sqrt{5}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-067","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":67,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): For every non-zero complex number $z$, $z^{-1} = \\frac{\\bar{z}}{|z|^2}$.\nReason (R): For every complex number $z$, $z\\bar{z} = |z|^2$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R: For $z = a + ib$, $z\\bar{z} = (a+ib)(a-ib) = a^2 + b^2 = |z|^2$. R is true.\nA: Divide $z\\bar{z} = |z|^2$ by $|z|^2$ (allowed because $z \\neq 0$). This gives $z \\cdot \\frac{\\bar z}{|z|^2} = 1$, so $z^{-1} = \\frac{\\bar z}{|z|^2}$. A is true.\nA follows directly from R, so R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-068","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":68,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"If $z = (3-4i)(5+12i)$, then $|z|$ equals","image":"","options":["65","63","17","60"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $z = (3-4i)(5+12i)$.\nFormula: $|z_1 z_2| = |z_1||z_2|$.\nSubstituting: $|3-4i| = \\sqrt{9+16} = 5$ and $|5+12i| = \\sqrt{25+144} = 13$.\nSo $|z| = 5 \\times 13 = 65$.\nCheck: $z = 15+36i-20i-48i^2 = 63+16i$, and $\\sqrt{63^2+16^2} = \\sqrt{4225} = 65$.\nOption 63 is only the real part of $z$, and 17 comes from wrongly adding the moduli.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-069","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":69,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Let $z=1+i$ and $w=2-i$. Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\n(A) $zw$\n(B) $z\\,\\bar{w}$\n(C) $z^{-1}$\n(D) $\\text{Re}(z\\,w^{-1})$\nList-II:\n(I) $\\frac{1}{5}$\n(II) $3+i$\n(III) $\\frac{1-i}{2}$\n(IV) $1+3i$","image":"","options":["A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I","A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) $zw=(1+i)(2-i)=2-i+2i+1=3+i$, which is (II).\n(B) $\\bar{w}=2+i$, so $z\\bar{w}=2+i+2i-1=1+3i$, which is (IV).\n(C) $z^{-1}=\\frac{1-i}{1^2+1^2}=\\frac{1-i}{2}$, which is (III).\n(D) $w^{-1}=\\frac{2+i}{5}$, so $zw^{-1}=\\frac{(1+i)(2+i)}{5}=\\frac{1+3i}{5}$. Its real part is $\\frac{1}{5}$, which is (I).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-070","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":70,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"If $(2x - y) + i(x + 3y) = 5 + 6i$, where $x$ and $y$ are real numbers, then $x + y$ equals:","image":"","options":["$4$","$2$","$3$","$7$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Two complex numbers are equal only when their real parts are equal and their imaginary parts are equal.\nReal parts: $2x - y = 5$, so $y = 2x - 5$.\nImaginary parts: $x + 3y = 6$, so $x + 6x - 15 = 6$, giving $7x = 21$ and $x = 3$.\nThen $y = 2(3) - 5 = 1$.\nSo $x + y = 4$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-071","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":71,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Let $z = 1+2i$. Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\n(A) $z\\bar z$\n(B) $z+\\bar z$\n(C) $z-\\bar z$\n(D) $\\frac{\\bar z}{z}$\nList-II:\n(i) $4i$\n(ii) 5\n(iii) 2\n(iv) $\\frac{-3-4i}{5}$","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(ii), B-(i), C-(iii), D-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Here $\\bar z = 1-2i$.\n(A) $z\\bar z = 1+4 = 5$, which is (ii).\n(B) $z+\\bar z = 2$, which is (iii).\n(C) $z-\\bar z = 4i$, which is (i).\n(D) Multiply numerator and denominator by $1-2i$: $\\frac{1-2i}{1+2i} = \\frac{(1-2i)^2}{5} = \\frac{1-4i-4}{5} = \\frac{-3-4i}{5}$, which is (iv).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-072","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":72,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Let $z=\\frac{2+i}{1-i}$. Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\n(P) $\\text{Re}\\,z$\n(Q) $\\text{Im}\\,z$\n(R) $|z|^2$\n(S) $\\text{Re}\\left(\\frac{1}{z}\\right)$\nList-II:\n(1) $\\frac{1}{5}$\n(2) $\\frac{1}{2}$\n(3) $\\frac{3}{2}$\n(4) $\\frac{5}{2}$","image":"","options":["P-3, Q-2, R-4, S-1","P-2, Q-3, R-1, S-4","P-1, Q-3, R-4, S-2","P-2, Q-3, R-4, S-1"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Multiply numerator and denominator by $1+i$:\n$z=\\frac{(2+i)(1+i)}{(1-i)(1+i)}=\\frac{2+3i+i^2}{2}=\\frac{1+3i}{2}$.\nSo $\\text{Re}\\,z=\\frac12$, matching (2), and $\\text{Im}\\,z=\\frac32$, matching (3).\n$|z|^2=\\frac{1+9}{4}=\\frac52$, matching (4).\n$\\frac1z=\\frac{2}{1+3i}=\\frac{2(1-3i)}{10}=\\frac{1-3i}{5}$, so its real part is $\\frac15$, matching (1).\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-073","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":73,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"The complex number $z$ satisfying $|z|+z = 2+i$ is","image":"","options":["$\\frac{3}{4}+i$","$-\\frac{3}{4}+i$","$\\frac{3}{4}-i$","$\\frac{5}{4}+i$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Let $z = x+iy$. Then $|z|+z = (\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}+x) + iy$.\nEquating imaginary parts: $y = 1$.\nEquating real parts: $\\sqrt{x^2+1} = 2-x$, which requires $x \\le 2$.\nSquaring: $x^2+1 = 4-4x+x^2$, so $x = \\frac34$.\nCheck: $|z| = \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{16}+1} = \\frac54$, and $\\frac54+\\frac34+i = 2+i$. Correct.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-074","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":74,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"The modulus of $\\frac{(3+4i)(1-i)}{2+i}$ is","image":"","options":["$\\sqrt{10}$","$5\\sqrt{2}$","$\\sqrt{5}$","$10$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Use $|z_1 z_2| = |z_1||z_2|$ and $\\left|\\frac{z_1}{z_2}\\right| = \\frac{|z_1|}{|z_2|}$.\nFind each modulus: $|3+4i| = 5$, $|1-i| = \\sqrt{2}$ and $|2+i| = \\sqrt{5}$.\nSo the modulus is $\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{5}} = \\sqrt{5}\\cdot\\sqrt{2} = \\sqrt{10}$.\nOption (2) forgets to divide by $|2+i|$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-075","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":75,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Using the identity $(z_1 + z_2)^2 = z_1^2 + 2z_1z_2 + z_2^2$, the value of $(2 + i)^2$ is","image":"","options":["$3 + 4i$","$5 + 4i$","$3 + 2i$","$4 + 4i$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Take $z_1 = 2$ and $z_2 = i$.\n$(2+i)^2 = 4 + 2(2)(i) + i^2 = 4 + 4i - 1$.\nSo $(2+i)^2 = 3 + 4i$.\nOption (2) comes from wrongly taking $i^2 = +1$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-076","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":76,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"The value of $\\left|\\frac{(2+i)^3}{(1-2i)^2}\\right|$ is","image":"","options":["$\\sqrt{5}$","5","1","$5\\sqrt{5}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Formulas: $|z_1/z_2| = |z_1|/|z_2|$ and $|z^n| = |z|^n$\nModuli: $|2+i| = \\sqrt5$ and $|1-2i| = \\sqrt5$\nSubstituting: the value is $\\frac{(\\sqrt5)^3}{(\\sqrt5)^2} = \\frac{5\\sqrt5}{5}$\nSo the value is $\\sqrt5$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-077","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":77,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"In the Argand plane, the complex number $z = -5 - 2i$ is plotted. In which quadrant does the point representing its conjugate $\\bar{z}$ lie?","image":"","options":["First quadrant","Second quadrant","Third quadrant","Fourth quadrant"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The number $z = -5 - 2i$ corresponds to the point $(-5, -2)$, which lies in the third quadrant.\nIts conjugate is $\\bar{z} = -5 + 2i$, which is the point $(-5, 2)$. Geometrically, this is the mirror image of $(-5, -2)$ in the real axis.\nThe point has a negative x-coordinate and a positive y-coordinate, so it lies in the second quadrant.\nThe third quadrant is the tempting wrong choice, but that is where $z$ itself lies, not $\\bar{z}$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-078","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":78,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"The value of $i + i^2 + i^3 + \\dots + i^{2026}$ is","image":"","options":["$0$","$i$","$-1 + i$","$-1$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Any four consecutive powers add to zero: $i + i^2 + i^3 + i^4 = i - 1 - i + 1 = 0$.\nWe have $2026 = 4(506) + 2$, so the first $2024$ terms make 506 complete cycles, each with sum 0.\nThe two terms left over are $i^{2025} + i^{2026} = i + i^2 = i - 1$.\nSo the sum is $-1 + i$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-079","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":79,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"In the Argand plane, which complex number is represented by the point $(0, -3)$?","image":"","options":["$-3 + 0i$","$3i$","$0 - 3i$","$-3 + i$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The point $(x, y)$ represents the complex number $x + iy$.\nHere $x = 0$ and $y = -3$, so the number is $0 + i(-3) = 0 - 3i$.\nThe point lies on the imaginary axis, below the origin.\nOption (1), $-3 + 0i$, is the point $(-3, 0)$ on the real axis. It interchanges the coordinates.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-080","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":80,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about multiplication of complex numbers are correct?\n(i) The product of two complex numbers is always a complex number.\n(ii) $z_1z_2=z_2z_1$ for all complex $z_1, z_2$.\n(iii) $(z_1z_2)z_3=z_1(z_2z_3)$ for all complex $z_1, z_2, z_3$.\n(iv) Every complex number has a multiplicative inverse.\n(v) $z(1+i0)=z$ for every complex $z$.\n(vi) If $z_1$ and $z_2$ both have non-zero imaginary parts, then $z_1z_2$ also has a non-zero imaginary part.","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: this is the closure law.\n(ii) Correct: this is the commutative law.\n(iii) Correct: this is the associative law.\n(iv) Incorrect: $0+i0$ has no inverse, since $a^2+b^2=0$.\n(v) Correct: $1+i0$ is the multiplicative identity.\n(vi) Incorrect: $i\\cdot i=-1$ has imaginary part 0.\nSo 4 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-081","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":81,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"The complex number $2 + 4i$ is plotted as point A in the Argand plane. What is the distance of A from the origin?","image":"","options":["$2\\sqrt{3}$","$6$","$20$","$2\\sqrt{5}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: $z = 2 + 4i$, so A is the point $(2, 4)$.\nFormula: distance from the origin $= |z| = \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2}$.\nSubstituting: $|z| = \\sqrt{2^2 + 4^2} = \\sqrt{4 + 16} = \\sqrt{20}$.\nSo the distance $= 2\\sqrt{5}$.\nOption (3), 20, is $|z|^2$, not $|z|$.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-082","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":82,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"The value of $\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^{13}\\left(i^{n} + i^{n+1}\\right)$ is","image":"","options":["$1 + i$","$-1 + i$","$i$","$-1 - i$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Factor each term: $i^n + i^{n+1} = i^n(1+i)$.\nSo the sum equals $(1+i)\\sum_{n=1}^{13} i^n$.\n$13 = 4(3)+1$, so three full cycles add to 0 and the one remaining term is $i^{13} = i$. Hence $\\sum_{n=1}^{13} i^n = i$.\nThe required sum is $(1+i)i = i + i^2 = -1 + i$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-083","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":83,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"The complex number $w$ such that $z + w = z$ for every complex number $z$ is","image":"","options":["$1 + i0$","$0 + i1$","$-z$","$0 + i0$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"This defines the additive identity.\nFor $z = a + ib$: $(a + ib) + (0 + i0) = a + ib$, so $w = 0 + i0$ works for every $z$.\n$1 + i0$ is the multiplicative identity. It satisfies $z \\cdot 1 = z$, not $z + 1 = z$.\n$-z$ gives $z + (-z) = 0$. It also changes with $z$, so it cannot be one fixed number.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-084","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":84,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): $\\sqrt{-4}\\times\\sqrt{-9}=6$\nReason (R): The rule $\\sqrt{a}\\times\\sqrt{b}=\\sqrt{ab}$ does not hold when both $a$ and $b$ are negative real numbers.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"By definition, $\\sqrt{-4}=2i$ and $\\sqrt{-9}=3i$.\nSo $\\sqrt{-4}\\times\\sqrt{-9}=6i^2=-6$, not 6. Hence A is false.\nGetting 6 would need $\\sqrt{(-4)(-9)}=\\sqrt{36}$. NCERT shows this rule fails when both numbers are negative (otherwise $i^2$ would equal 1). So R is true.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-085","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":85,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (complex number) with List-II (its multiplicative inverse):\nList-I\n(P) $i$\n(Q) $1 + i$\n(R) $1 - 2i$\n(S) $2 - i$\nList-II\n(1) $\\frac{1 - i}{2}$\n(2) $-i$\n(3) $\\frac{2 + i}{5}$\n(4) $\\frac{1 + 2i}{5}$","image":"","options":["P-2, Q-1, R-3, S-4","P-2, Q-1, R-4, S-3","P-1, Q-2, R-4, S-3","P-4, Q-3, R-2, S-1"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Use $z^{-1} = \\frac{\\bar z}{|z|^2}$ for each number.\n(P) $\\bar z = -i$, $|z|^2 = 1$, so the inverse is $-i$ → (2).\n(Q) $\\frac{1 - i}{2}$ → (1).\n(R) $\\frac{1 + 2i}{1 + 4} = \\frac{1+2i}{5}$ → (4).\n(S) $\\frac{2 + i}{4 + 1} = \\frac{2+i}{5}$ → (3).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-086","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":86,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"The multiplicative inverse of $2i$ is","image":"","options":["$2i$","$\\frac{i}{2}$","$-\\frac{i}{2}$","$-2i$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"$\\frac{1}{2i} = \\frac{1}{2i} \\times \\frac{i}{i} = \\frac{i}{2i^2} = \\frac{i}{-2} = -\\frac{i}{2}$.\nCheck: $2i \\times \\left(-\\frac{i}{2}\\right) = -i^2 = 1$.\nOption (2) misses the sign that comes from $i^2 = -1$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-087","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":87,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Subtraction of complex numbers is commutative, i.e., $z_1 - z_2 = z_2 - z_1$ for all complex numbers $z_1, z_2$.\nStatement II: For every complex number $z$, $z - z = 0 + i0$.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I: Take $z_1 = 6 + 3i$ and $z_2 = 2 - i$.\nThen $z_1 - z_2 = 4 + 4i$, but $z_2 - z_1 = -4 - 4i$. These are unequal, so Statement I is false.\nStatement II: $z - z = z + (-z) = 0 + i0$, using the additive inverse. So Statement II is true.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-088","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":88,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are true for EVERY non-zero complex number $z$?\n(a) $z^{-1} = \\frac{\\bar{z}}{|z|^2}$\n(b) $|z| = |\\bar{z}|$\n(c) $z - \\bar{z}$ is either zero or purely imaginary\n(d) $|z^2| = |z|^2$\n(e) $\\overline{(\\bar{z})} = z$\n(f) $z^2 = |z|^2$\n(g) If $|z| = 1$, then $\\frac{1}{z} = \\bar{z}$\n(h) Re$(z^2) = ($Re $z)^2$","image":"","options":["5","6","7","8"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Let $z = a + ib$.\n(a) True, since $z\\bar{z} = |z|^2$.\n(b) True: both equal $\\sqrt{a^2 + b^2}$.\n(c) True: $z - \\bar{z} = 2ib$.\n(d) True, since $|z_1 z_2| = |z_1||z_2|$.\n(e) True.\n(f) False: $z^2 = a^2 - b^2 + 2abi$, while $|z|^2 = a^2 + b^2$. They are equal only when $b = 0$.\n(g) True, from (a) with $|z| = 1$.\n(h) False: Re$(z^2) = a^2 - b^2$, which differs from $a^2$ when $b \\ne 0$.\nSo 6 statements are true.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-089","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":89,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"The number of complex numbers $z$ satisfying $\\bar{z} = z^2$ is:","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Let $z = x + iy$ with $x, y$ real. Then $x - iy = (x^2 - y^2) + i(2xy)$.\nReal parts: $x = x^2 - y^2$. Imaginary parts: $-y = 2xy$, i.e. $y(2x + 1) = 0$.\nCase $y = 0$: $x = x^2$, so $x = 0$ or $x = 1$. This gives $z = 0$ and $z = 1$.\nCase $x = -\\frac{1}{2}$: $-\\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{1}{4} - y^2$, so $y = \\pm\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$. This gives two more values of $z$.\nTotal: 4 complex numbers.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-090","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":90,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): $\\sqrt{-4}\\,\\sqrt{-9}=\\sqrt{36}=6$.\nReason (R): For any positive real number $a$, $\\sqrt{-a}=\\sqrt{a}\\,i$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"R is true: $\\sqrt{-a}=\\sqrt{a}\\sqrt{-1}=\\sqrt{a}\\,i$ for $a>0$.\nA is false: using R, $\\sqrt{-4}\\sqrt{-9}=(2i)(3i)=6i^2=-6$, not $6$.\nThe error in A is the step $\\sqrt{a}\\sqrt{b}=\\sqrt{ab}$, which fails when both $a$ and $b$ are negative. Applying it to $\\sqrt{-1}\\sqrt{-1}$ would give $i^2=1$, a contradiction.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-091","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":91,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The conjugate of $\\frac{1}{2-i}$ is $\\frac{2+i}{5}$.\nReason (R): For complex numbers $z_1, z_2$ with $z_2 \\ne 0$, $\\overline{\\left(\\frac{z_1}{z_2}\\right)} = \\frac{\\bar{z}_1}{\\bar{z}_2}$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"R is one of the conjugate properties listed in NCERT, so R is true.\nUsing R: $\\overline{\\left(\\frac{1}{2-i}\\right)} = \\frac{1}{2+i}$.\nRationalise: $\\frac{1}{2+i} = \\frac{2-i}{(2+i)(2-i)} = \\frac{2-i}{5}$.\nThe value $\\frac{2+i}{5}$ is $\\frac{1}{2-i}$ itself, not its conjugate. So A is false.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-092","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":92,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following equations has $3i$ and $-3i$ as its solutions?","image":"","options":["$x^2 + 9 = 0$","$x^2 - 9 = 0$","$x^2 + 3 = 0$","$x^2 + 6x + 9 = 0$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Square each value: $(3i)^2 = 9i^2 = -9$ and $(-3i)^2 = 9i^2 = -9$.\nSo both satisfy $x^2 = -9$, that is, $x^2 + 9 = 0$.\n$x^2 - 9 = 0$ has the real roots $\\pm 3$.\n$x^2 + 3 = 0$ has the roots $\\pm\\sqrt{3}i$.\n$x^2 + 6x + 9 = 0$ has the repeated root $-3$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-093","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":93,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Let $z_1=1+2i$. A complex number $z_2$ is such that $z_1z_2$ is purely real and $\\text{Re}(z_1z_2)=10$. Then $z_2$ is","image":"","options":["$2-4i$","$2+4i$","$-2+4i$","$4-2i$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Let $z_2=x+iy$. Then $z_1z_2=(x-2y)+i(y+2x)$.\nPurely real means $y+2x=0$, so $y=-2x$.\nThe real part is $x-2y=x+4x=5x=10$, so $x=2$ and $y=-4$.\nThus $z_2=2-4i$. Check: $(1+2i)(2-4i)=2-4i+4i+8=10$.\nOption (3) also gives a real product, but it equals $-10$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-094","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":94,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are equal to $-1$?\n$i^{102}$, $i^{-2}$, $i^{75}$, $i^{-3}$, $i^{200}$","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Use $i^{4k}=1$, $i^{4k+1}=i$, $i^{4k+2}=-1$ and $i^{4k+3}=-i$. Find each exponent mod 4.\n$i^{102}$: $102=4(25)+2$, so it equals $-1$. ✔\n$i^{-2}=\\frac{1}{i^2}=-1$. ✔\n$i^{75}$: $75=4(18)+3$, so it equals $-i$. ✘\n$i^{-3}=\\frac{1}{i^3}=\\frac{1}{-i}=i$. ✘\n$i^{200}$: $200=4(50)$, so it equals $1$. ✘\nTwo of them equal $-1$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-095","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":95,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): $(1+i)(1-i)$ is a real number.\nReason (R): The product of any two complex numbers having non-zero imaginary parts is always a real number.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A: $(1+i)(1-i)=1-i+i-i^2=1+1=2$, which is real. So A is true.\nR: Counterexample: $(1+i)(2+i)=2+i+2i+i^2=1+3i$, which is not real. So R is false.\nThe product is real here only because $1-i$ is the conjugate of $1+i$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-096","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":96,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"For complex numbers $z_1$ and $z_2$, which of the following statements is NOT true in general?","image":"","options":["$\\overline{z_1 + z_2} = \\bar{z_1} + \\bar{z_2}$","$|z_1 z_2| = |z_1||z_2|$","$|z_1 + z_2| = |z_1| + |z_2|$","$z_1 \\bar{z_1} = |z_1|^2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) True: the conjugate of a sum is the sum of the conjugates.\n(2) True: the modulus of a product is the product of the moduli.\n(3) Not true in general: take $z_1 = 1$ and $z_2 = -1$. Then $|z_1+z_2| = 0$, but $|z_1|+|z_2| = 2$.\n(4) True: $(a+ib)(a-ib) = a^2+b^2 = |z_1|^2$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-097","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":97,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) The set of complex numbers is closed under addition.\n(ii) The additive inverse of $a + ib$ is $a - ib$.\n(iii) $(z_1 + z_2) + z_3 = z_1 + (z_2 + z_3)$ for all complex $z_1, z_2, z_3$.\n(iv) $z_1 - z_2$ is defined as $z_1 + (-z_2)$.\n(v) $z_1 - z_2 = z_2 - z_1$ for all complex $z_1, z_2$.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: $(a+c) + i(b+d)$ is again a complex number.\n(ii) Incorrect: the additive inverse is $-a - ib$. The number $a - ib$ is the conjugate.\n(iii) Correct: this is the associative law of addition.\n(iv) Correct: this is the definition of difference.\n(v) Incorrect: subtraction is not commutative.\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-098","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":98,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"The number of integer values of $p$ for which the equation $x^2 + px + 9 = 0$ has no real roots (that is, its roots are non-real complex numbers) is:","image":"","options":["11","13","12","10"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The roots are non-real when $D < 0$.\nHere $D = p^2 - 4(1)(9) = p^2 - 36$.\nSo we need $p^2 < 36$, which means $-6 < p < 6$.\nThe integers in this range are $-5, -4, \\ldots, 4, 5$, which is 11 values.\nAt $p = \\pm 6$, $D = 0$ and the roots are real and equal, so these are excluded.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-099","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":99,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): There is no real number $x$ for which $x + ix = 2 + 3i$.\nReason (R): Two complex numbers are equal if and only if their real parts are equal and their imaginary parts are equal.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true: this is exactly NCERT's definition of equality of complex numbers.\nA: Since $x$ is real, equating real parts gives $x = 2$, and equating imaginary parts gives $x = 3$.\nThese two values contradict each other, so no real $x$ satisfies the equation. Hence A is true.\nThe contradiction arises only because we apply the equality rule stated in R, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-100","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":100,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"The least positive integer $n$ for which $(1+i)^{2n} = (1-i)^{2n}$ is","image":"","options":["$1$","$2$","$4$","$8$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"$(1+i)^2 = 1 + 2i - 1 = 2i$ and $(1-i)^2 = 1 - 2i - 1 = -2i$.\nSo the condition becomes $(2i)^n = (-2i)^n$.\nDividing both sides by $(2i)^n$ (which is non-zero) gives $(-1)^n = 1$, so $n$ must be even.\nThe least positive even $n$ is 2.\nCheck $n = 1$: $2i \\neq -2i$, so $n = 1$ fails.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-101","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":101,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"The multiplicative inverse of $3+4i$ is","image":"","options":["$\\frac{3+4i}{25}$","$\\frac{3-4i}{25}$","$\\frac{3-4i}{5}$","$\\frac{-3+4i}{25}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"For $z=a+ib\\neq0$, the inverse is $z^{-1}=\\frac{a-ib}{a^2+b^2}$.\nHere $a^2+b^2=9+16=25$, so $z^{-1}=\\frac{3-4i}{25}$.\nCheck: $(3+4i)(3-4i)=9+16=25$, so $(3+4i)\\cdot\\frac{3-4i}{25}=1$.\nOption (3) divides by $|z|=5$ instead of $|z|^2=25$, so it is wrong.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-102","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":102,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Real numbers $x$ and $y$ are such that $(x - iy)(1 + 2i)$ is the conjugate of $7 - 4i$. Then $(x, y)$ is:","image":"","options":["$\\left(-\\frac{1}{5}, \\frac{18}{5}\\right)$","$(3, -2)$","$(2, 3)$","$(3, 2)$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The conjugate of $7 - 4i$ is $7 + 4i$.\nExpand: $(x - iy)(1 + 2i) = x + 2ix - iy - 2i^2 y = (x + 2y) + i(2x - y)$.\nEquate parts: $x + 2y = 7$ and $2x - y = 4$.\nFrom the first, $x = 7 - 2y$. Then $14 - 4y - y = 4$, so $y = 2$ and $x = 3$.\nOption (1) is what you get if you equate to $7 - 4i$ itself, forgetting to take the conjugate.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-103","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":103,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"If $z$ is a complex number with $|z| = 2$, then $\\left| z + \\frac{4}{\\bar{z}} \\right|$ equals","image":"","options":["4","2","0","8"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $|z| = 2$, so $z\\bar{z} = |z|^2 = 4$.\nHence $\\bar{z} = \\frac{4}{z}$, which gives $\\frac{4}{\\bar{z}} = z$.\nSo $z + \\frac{4}{\\bar{z}} = 2z$, and its modulus is $2|z| = 4$.\nOption (3) comes from wrongly taking $\\frac{4}{\\bar{z}} = -z$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-104","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":104,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Let $z = a+ib$, where $a$ and $b$ are real. Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\n(A) $|\\bar{z}|$\n(B) $z\\bar{z}$\n(C) $z+\\bar{z}$\n(D) $z-\\bar{z}$\nList-II:\n(i) $2i \\, \\text{Im } z$\n(ii) $|z|$\n(iii) $2 \\, \\text{Re } z$\n(iv) $|z|^2$","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) $|\\bar{z}| = \\sqrt{a^2+(-b)^2} = |z|$, so (ii).\n(B) $z\\bar{z} = a^2+b^2 = |z|^2$, so (iv).\n(C) $z+\\bar{z} = 2a = 2 \\, \\text{Re } z$, so (iii).\n(D) $z-\\bar{z} = 2ib = 2i \\, \\text{Im } z$, so (i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-105","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":105,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): $i^{4k} = 1$ for every integer $k$, including negative integers.\nReason (R): $i^4 = 1$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R: $i^4 = (i^2)^2 = (-1)^2 = 1$, so R is true.\nA: $i^{4k} = (i^4)^k = 1^k = 1$ for every integer $k$. For negative $k$, $1^k = \\frac{1}{1^{|k|}} = 1$ also holds. For example, $i^{-4} = \\frac{1}{i^4} = 1$. So A is true.\nThe result in A follows directly from R, so R is the correct explanation of A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-106","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":106,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: $i \\cdot i^2 \\cdot i^3 \\cdots i^{50} = -i$.\nStatement II: $i^{-n} = i^{n}$ for every integer $n$.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I: the product equals $i^{1+2+\\dots+50}$. The exponent is $\\frac{50 \\times 51}{2} = 1275$.\nSince $1275 = 4(318)+3$, the product is $i^3 = -i$. Statement I is true.\nStatement II: take $n = 1$. Then $i^{-1} = \\frac{1}{i} = -i$, which is not equal to $i^1 = i$. Statement II is false.\nThe two sides agree only when $n$ is even.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-107","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":107,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is a purely imaginary number?","image":"","options":["$\\sqrt{-16}$","$\\sqrt{-4}\\times\\sqrt{-9}$","$5 + i\\,0$","$i^2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(1) $\\sqrt{-16} = \\sqrt{16}\\,i = 4i$, which is $0 + 4i$, so it is purely imaginary.\n(2) $\\sqrt{-4}\\times\\sqrt{-9} = (2i)(3i) = 6i^2 = -6$, which is real.\n(3) $5 + i\\,0 = 5$, which is real.\n(4) $i^2 = -1$, which is real.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-108","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":108,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"The value of $(-\\sqrt{2} + \\sqrt{-3})(\\sqrt{3} - \\sqrt{-8})$ in the form $a + ib$ is","image":"","options":["$\\sqrt{6} + 7i$","$-3\\sqrt{6} + 7i$","$\\sqrt{6} - 7i$","$-\\sqrt{6} + 7i$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"First convert the roots: $\\sqrt{-3} = \\sqrt{3}i$ and $\\sqrt{-8} = 2\\sqrt{2}i$.\nThe product becomes $(-\\sqrt{2} + \\sqrt{3}i)(\\sqrt{3} - 2\\sqrt{2}i)$.\nExpand: $-\\sqrt{6} + 4i + 3i - 2\\sqrt{6}i^2$.\nWith $i^2 = -1$: $-\\sqrt{6} + 7i + 2\\sqrt{6} = \\sqrt{6} + 7i$.\nOption (2) comes from wrongly writing $\\sqrt{-3}\\sqrt{-8} = \\sqrt{24}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-109","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":109,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"The modulus of the product $(3+4i)(5-12i)$ is","image":"","options":["65","63","17","33"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Formula: $|z_1z_2| = |z_1||z_2|$\nModuli: $|3+4i| = \\sqrt{9+16} = 5$ and $|5-12i| = \\sqrt{25+144} = 13$\nSo $|z_1z_2| = 5 \\times 13 = 65$\nCheck: $(3+4i)(5-12i) = 15-36i+20i+48 = 63-16i$, and $\\sqrt{63^2+16^2} = \\sqrt{4225} = 65$. The option 63 is only the real part of the product.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-110","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":110,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) $\\sqrt{-3} \\times \\sqrt{-12} = -6$\n(ii) $\\sqrt{-2} \\times \\sqrt{8} = 4i$\n(iii) $\\sqrt{-5} \\times \\sqrt{-5} = 5$\n(iv) $\\sqrt{(-4)(-9)} = \\sqrt{-4} \\times \\sqrt{-9}$\n(v) $\\sqrt{0} \\times \\sqrt{-7} = 0$","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: $(\\sqrt{3}i)(2\\sqrt{3}i) = 6i^2 = -6$.\n(ii) Correct: $(\\sqrt{2}i)(2\\sqrt{2}) = 4i$.\n(iii) Incorrect: $(\\sqrt{5}i)^2 = -5$, not $5$.\n(iv) Incorrect: the left side is $6$, but the right side is $(2i)(3i) = -6$.\n(v) Correct: if either factor is zero, the product is $0$.\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-111","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":111,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements is INCORRECT? (k is any integer)","image":"","options":["$i^{4k+3} = -i$","$i^{-2} = -1$","$i^{-3} = -i$","$i^{4k} = 1$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: $i^{4k+3} = (i^4)^k\\, i^3 = -i$.\n(2) Correct: $i^{-2} = \\frac{1}{i^2} = \\frac{1}{-1} = -1$.\n(3) Incorrect: $i^{-3} = \\frac{1}{i^3} = \\frac{1}{-i} = \\frac{i}{-i\\cdot i} = \\frac{i}{1} = i$, not $-i$.\n(4) Correct: $i^{4k} = (i^4)^k = 1$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-112","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":112,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (mathematician) with List-II (contribution related to complex numbers).\nList-I:\n(a) Mahavira\n(b) Bhaskara\n(c) Euler\n(d) W. R. Hamilton\nList-II:\n(i) First introduced the symbol $i$ for $\\sqrt{-1}$\n(ii) Wrote in Ganitasara Sangraha that a negative quantity is not a square and so has no square root\n(iii) Regarded $a+ib$ as an ordered pair of real numbers $(a, b)$ (about 1830)\n(iv) Wrote in Bijaganita (1150) that there is no square root of a negative quantity","image":"","options":["(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(a) Mahavira (850) stated the difficulty in Ganitasara Sangraha, so (a)-(ii).\n(b) Bhaskara wrote Bijaganita in 1150, so (b)-(iv).\n(c) Euler was the first to use the symbol $i$ for $\\sqrt{-1}$, so (c)-(i).\n(d) Hamilton (about 1830) defined $a+ib$ as the ordered pair $(a, b)$, so (d)-(iii).\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-113","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":113,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\n(A) Multiplicative inverse of $1 - i$\n(B) Multiplicative inverse of $2i$\n(C) $\\frac{1 - i}{1 + i}$\n(D) $\\frac{2 + i}{2 - i}$\nList-II:\n(I) $-i$\n(II) $\\frac{1 + i}{2}$\n(III) $\\frac{3 + 4i}{5}$\n(IV) $-\\frac{i}{2}$","image":"","options":["A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III","A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III","A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) $\\frac{1}{1 - i} = \\frac{1 + i}{(1 - i)(1 + i)} = \\frac{1 + i}{2}$, so A matches II.\n(B) $\\frac{1}{2i} = \\frac{-i}{2i(-i)} = -\\frac{i}{2}$, so B matches IV.\n(C) $\\frac{(1 - i)^2}{2} = \\frac{-2i}{2} = -i$, so C matches I.\n(D) $\\frac{(2 + i)^2}{5} = \\frac{3 + 4i}{5}$, so D matches III.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-114","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":114,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): If $z_1$ and $z_2$ both have non-zero imaginary parts, then $\\text{Re}(z_1z_2)\\neq \\text{Re}\\,z_1\\cdot\\text{Re}\\,z_2$.\nReason (R): $\\text{Re}(z_1z_2)=\\text{Re}\\,z_1\\,\\text{Re}\\,z_2-\\text{Im}\\,z_1\\,\\text{Im}\\,z_2$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R: Let $z_1=a+ib$ and $z_2=c+id$. Then $z_1z_2=(ac-bd)+i(ad+bc)$, so $\\text{Re}(z_1z_2)=ac-bd$. R is true.\nA: If $b\\neq0$ and $d\\neq0$, then $bd\\neq0$. So $ac-bd\\neq ac$, and A is true.\nA follows directly from R, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-115","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":115,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following numbers are purely real?\n(i) $i^2$\n(ii) $\\sqrt{-2}\\times\\sqrt{-8}$\n(iii) $\\sqrt{-9}$\n(iv) $(\\sqrt{-3})^2$\n(v) $i^3$","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) $i^2 = -1$, so it is real.\n(ii) $(\\sqrt{2}i)(2\\sqrt{2}i) = 4i^2 = -4$, so it is real.\n(iii) $\\sqrt{-9} = 3i$, which is purely imaginary, not real.\n(iv) $(\\sqrt{3}i)^2 = 3i^2 = -3$, so it is real.\n(v) $i^3 = -i$, which is not real.\nThree of the numbers are purely real.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-116","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":116,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Let $z = 2 + 4i$. The points representing $z$, $\\bar{z}$, $-z$ and $-\\bar{z}$ are plotted in the Argand plane. What is the area of the quadrilateral having these four points as vertices?","image":"","options":["8 sq units","16 sq units","32 sq units","64 sq units"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $z = 2 + 4i$ gives P(2, 4), $\\bar{z} = 2 - 4i$ gives Q(2, -4), $-z = -2 - 4i$ gives R(-2, -4), and $-\\bar{z} = -2 + 4i$ gives S(-2, 4).\nThese four points are symmetric about both axes, so PQRS is a rectangle.\nPQ = 4 - (-4) = 8 (vertical side) and QR = 2 - (-2) = 4 (horizontal side).\nArea $= 8 \\times 4 = 32$ sq units. In general, the area is $4|xy|$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-117","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":117,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"If $x$ and $y$ are real numbers such that $\\left(2x + i(y - 3)\\right) + (5 - 4i) = 9 + 2i$, then $x + y$ equals","image":"","options":["5","7","11","13"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Left side: $(2x + 5) + i(y - 3 - 4) = (2x + 5) + i(y - 7)$.\nEquate real parts: $2x + 5 = 9$, so $x = 2$.\nEquate imaginary parts: $y - 7 = 2$, so $y = 9$.\nSo $x + y = 2 + 9 = 11$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-118","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":118,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"If $z = x + iy$ (with $x, y$ real) satisfies $z^2 = -3 + 4i$, then $z$ equals","image":"","options":["$\\pm(1+2i)$","$\\pm(2+i)$","$\\pm(1-2i)$","$\\pm(2-i)$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Expand: $(x+iy)^2 = x^2 - y^2 + 2xyi$. Equating parts gives $x^2 - y^2 = -3$ and $2xy = 4$.\nTaking moduli, $|z|^2 = |z^2| = \\sqrt{9+16} = 5$, so $x^2 + y^2 = 5$.\nAdding and subtracting: $x^2 = 1$ and $y^2 = 4$.\nSince $xy = 2 > 0$, $x$ and $y$ have the same sign. So $(x, y) = (1, 2)$ or $(-1, -2)$, i.e., $z = \\pm(1+2i)$.\nCheck: $(1+2i)^2 = 1 + 4i - 4 = -3 + 4i$. In contrast, $(2+i)^2 = 3 + 4i$, which does not match.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-119","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":119,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"The discriminant of the quadratic equation $2x^2 + 3x + 5 = 0$ is:","image":"","options":["$-31$","$31$","$49$","$-40$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $a = 2$, $b = 3$, $c = 5$.\nFormula: $D = b^2 - 4ac$.\nSubstituting: $D = 9 - 40 = -31$.\nSo $D = -31 < 0$. The equation has no real roots, and complex numbers are needed to solve it.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-120","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":120,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Let $z = a + ib$ be a non-zero complex number.\nStatement I: $\\text{Re}\\left(\\frac{1}{z}\\right)$ is positive if and only if $\\text{Re}(z)$ is positive.\nStatement II: $\\text{Im}\\left(\\frac{1}{z}\\right)$ always has the same sign as $\\text{Im}(z)$.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Write $\\frac{1}{z} = \\frac{a - ib}{a^2 + b^2} = \\frac{a}{a^2 + b^2} - \\frac{b}{a^2 + b^2}i$.\nStatement I: $a^2 + b^2 > 0$, so $\\text{Re}\\left(\\frac{1}{z}\\right)$ has the same sign as $a$. True.\nStatement II: $\\text{Im}\\left(\\frac{1}{z}\\right) = -\\frac{b}{a^2 + b^2}$, which has the opposite sign to $b$ whenever $b \\neq 0$. False. For example, $\\frac{1}{i} = -i$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-121","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":121,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Let $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ be complex numbers with $|\\beta| = 1$. Which of the following is always equal to $1 - \\bar{\\alpha}\\beta$?","image":"","options":["$\\bar{\\beta}(\\beta - \\alpha)$","$\\frac{\\beta - \\alpha}{\\beta}$","$\\beta(\\bar{\\beta} - \\bar{\\alpha})$","$1 - |\\alpha|$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Since $|\\beta|=1$, we have $\\beta\\bar{\\beta} = |\\beta|^2 = 1$.\nSo $1 - \\bar{\\alpha}\\beta = \\beta\\bar{\\beta} - \\beta\\bar{\\alpha} = \\beta(\\bar{\\beta} - \\bar{\\alpha})$.\nThis also gives $|1-\\bar{\\alpha}\\beta| = |\\beta|\\,|\\overline{\\beta-\\alpha}| = |\\beta - \\alpha|$.\nOption (1) expands to $1 - \\alpha\\bar{\\beta}$, which is the conjugate of the required expression, not the expression itself.\nOption (2) also expands to $1 - \\alpha\\bar{\\beta}$, so it fails for the same reason.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-122","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":122,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"The value of $\\left[i^{17} - \\left(\\frac{1}{i}\\right)^{34}\\right]^2$ is","image":"","options":["$2$","$-2i$","$0$","$2i$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"$17 = 4(4)+1$, so $i^{17} = i$.\n$\\frac{1}{i} = -i$, so $\\left(\\frac{1}{i}\\right)^{34} = (-i)^{34} = i^{34}$. Since $34 = 4(8)+2$, this equals $-1$.\nThe bracket becomes $i - (-1) = 1 + i$.\nSquaring: $(1+i)^2 = 1 + 2i + i^2 = 2i$.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-123","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":123,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following hold for all complex numbers $z_1$ and $z_2$?\n(i) $(z_1+z_2)^2=z_1^2+2z_1z_2+z_2^2$\n(ii) $(z_1-z_2)^3=z_1^3-3z_1^2z_2+3z_1z_2^2-z_2^3$\n(iii) $z_1^2-z_2^2=(z_1+z_2)(z_1-z_2)$\n(iv) $(z_1+z_2)^3=z_1^3+z_2^3+3z_1z_2(z_1+z_2)$\n(v) $(z_1-z_2)^2=z_1^2-z_2^2-2z_1z_2$","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) True: this follows from the distributive and commutative laws.\n(ii) True: this is the standard cube identity.\n(iii) True: $(z_1+z_2)(z_1-z_2)=z_1^2-z_1z_2+z_2z_1-z_2^2=z_1^2-z_2^2$.\n(iv) True: expanding the right side gives $z_1^3+3z_1^2z_2+3z_1z_2^2+z_2^3$.\n(v) False: the correct form is $z_1^2-2z_1z_2+z_2^2$. For example, $z_1=0$, $z_2=1$ gives $1\\neq-1$.\nSo 4 identities hold.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-124","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":124,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\n(A) $(1+i)^2$\n(B) $\\frac{1+i}{1-i}$\n(C) $(1-i)^4$\n(D) $(2+i)(2-i)$\nList-II:\n(I) $i$\n(II) $5$\n(III) $2i$\n(IV) $-4$","image":"","options":["A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-I, B-III, C-IV, D-II","A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV","A-IV, B-I, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) $(1+i)^2 = 1 + 2i + i^2 = 2i$, so A matches III.\n(B) Multiply numerator and denominator by $1+i$: $\\frac{(1+i)^2}{1-i^2} = \\frac{2i}{2} = i$, so B matches I.\n(C) $(1-i)^2 = -2i$, so $(1-i)^4 = (-2i)^2 = 4i^2 = -4$, and C matches IV.\n(D) $(2+i)(2-i) = 4 - i^2 = 5$, so D matches II.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-125","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":125,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II ($k$ is an integer).\nList-I:\n(P) $|3-4i|$\n(Q) $\\text{Re}\\,(1+i)^2$\n(R) $\\text{Im}\\,(1+i)^2$\n(S) $i^{4k+1}\\cdot i^{3}$\nList-II:\n(1) 0\n(2) 1\n(3) 2\n(4) 5","image":"","options":["P-4, Q-3, R-1, S-2","P-4, Q-1, R-3, S-2","P-5, Q-1, R-2, S-3","P-3, Q-1, R-3, S-4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(P) $|3-4i|=\\sqrt{9+16}=5$, which matches (4).\n(Q) and (R): $(1+i)^2=1+2i+i^2=2i$. So the real part is 0, matching (1), and the imaginary part is 2, matching (3).\n(S) $i^{4k+1}\\cdot i^3=i^{4k+4}=1$, which matches (2).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-126","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":126,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: For all complex numbers $z_1$ and $z_2$, $|z_1z_2|=|z_1||z_2|$.\nStatement II: For all complex numbers $z_1$ and $z_2$, $|z_1+z_2|=|z_1|+|z_2|$.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is true. It is a standard NCERT result for modulus.\nCheck with an example: $|(1+i)(1-i)|=|2|=2$, and $\\sqrt2\\cdot\\sqrt2=2$.\nStatement II is false. Take $z_1=1$ and $z_2=-1$. Then $|z_1+z_2|=0$, but $|z_1|+|z_2|=2$.\nThe modulus of a sum is not, in general, the sum of the moduli.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-127","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":127,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"For non-zero complex numbers $z$, $z_1$, $z_2$, how many of the following statements are always true?\n(i) $\\overline{z_1z_2} = \\bar z_1\\,\\bar z_2$\n(ii) $|z_1-z_2| = |z_1|-|z_2|$\n(iii) $z-\\bar z$ is either zero or purely imaginary\n(iv) $|z_1+z_2|^2 = |z_1|^2+|z_2|^2+2\\,\\text{Re}(z_1\\bar z_2)$\n(v) $|\\bar z|^2 = z^2$","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) True: this is the standard rule for the conjugate of a product.\n(ii) False: take $z_1 = i$ and $z_2 = -i$. Then $|z_1-z_2| = 2$, but $|z_1|-|z_2| = 0$.\n(iii) True: $z-\\bar z = 2iy$.\n(iv) True: $(z_1+z_2)(\\bar z_1+\\bar z_2) = |z_1|^2+|z_2|^2+z_1\\bar z_2+\\overline{z_1\\bar z_2}$, and the last two terms add up to $2\\,\\text{Re}(z_1\\bar z_2)$.\n(v) False: for $z = i$, $|\\bar z|^2 = 1$ but $z^2 = -1$.\nSo 3 statements are always true.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-128","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":128,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"If $x$ and $y$ are real numbers satisfying $\\frac{x}{1+i} + \\frac{y}{1-2i} = 1 + i$, then $x + y$ equals:","image":"","options":["$2$","$4$","$\\frac{16}{3}$","$\\frac{8}{3}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Rationalise each term: $\\frac{x}{1+i} = \\frac{x(1-i)}{2}$ and $\\frac{y}{1-2i} = \\frac{y(1+2i)}{5}$.\nSo the left side is $\\left(\\frac{x}{2} + \\frac{y}{5}\\right) + i\\left(-\\frac{x}{2} + \\frac{2y}{5}\\right)$.\nEquate parts: $\\frac{x}{2} + \\frac{y}{5} = 1$ and $-\\frac{x}{2} + \\frac{2y}{5} = 1$.\nAdding the two equations: $\\frac{3y}{5} = 2$, so $y = \\frac{10}{3}$. Then $\\frac{x}{2} = 1 - \\frac{2}{3} = \\frac{1}{3}$, so $x = \\frac{2}{3}$.\nSo $x + y = 4$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-129","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":129,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["The multiplicative inverse of $3+4i$ is $\\frac{3}{25}-\\frac{4}{25}i$","For every complex number $z$, $z\\bar{z}=|z|^2$","For complex numbers $z_1, z_2$, $\\overline{z_1 z_2}=\\bar{z}_1\\,\\bar{z}_2$","The multiplicative inverse of $-i$ is $-i$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: $z^{-1}=\\frac{\\bar z}{|z|^2}=\\frac{3-4i}{25}$.\n(2) Correct: $(a+ib)(a-ib)=a^2+b^2=|z|^2$.\n(3) Correct: this is a standard property of conjugates.\n(4) Incorrect: $(-i)(-i)=i^2=-1$, not 1. The true inverse is $\\frac{1}{-i}=\\frac{i}{-i^2}=i$, since $(-i)(i)=-i^2=1$.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-130","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":130,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"The value of $(2 - i)^3$ in the form $a + ib$ is","image":"","options":["$2 - 11i$","$2 + 11i$","$14 - 11i$","$2 - 13i$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Use $(z_1 - z_2)^3 = z_1^3 - 3z_1^2z_2 + 3z_1z_2^2 - z_2^3$ with $z_1 = 2$ and $z_2 = i$.\n$(2-i)^3 = 8 - 3(4)(i) + 3(2)(i^2) - i^3$.\nSubstitute $i^2 = -1$ and $i^3 = -i$: $8 - 12i - 6 + i$.\nThis simplifies to $2 - 11i$.\nCheck: $(2-i)^2 = 3 - 4i$, and $(3-4i)(2-i) = 6 - 3i - 8i - 4 = 2 - 11i$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-131","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":131,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\n(A) $i^{-3}$\n(B) $i^{-50}$\n(C) $i^{243}$\n(D) $i^{-104}$\nList-II:\n(I) $1$\n(II) $-1$\n(III) $i$\n(IV) $-i$\nChoose the correct answer.","image":"","options":["A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I","A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-IV, B-I, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) $-3 = 4(-1) + 1$, so $i^{-3} = i$, giving A-III.\n(B) $-50 = 4(-13) + 2$, so $i^{-50} = -1$, giving B-II.\n(C) $243 = 4(60) + 3$, so $i^{243} = -i$, giving C-IV.\n(D) $-104 = 4(-26)$, so $i^{-104} = 1$, giving D-I.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-132","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":132,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) For $z = a + ib \\neq 0$, the real part of $\\frac{1}{z}$ is $\\frac{a}{a^2+b^2}$.\n(ii) $\\left|\\frac{1}{z}\\right| = \\frac{1}{|z|}$ for every non-zero $z$.\n(iii) $\\overline{\\left(\\frac{z_1}{z_2}\\right)} = \\frac{\\bar{z}_1}{\\bar{z}_2}$ for $z_2 \\neq 0$.\n(iv) $\\frac{1 + i}{1 - i} = -i$.\n(v) For $z = i$, $z^{-1} = \\bar{z}$.","image":"","options":["2","3","5","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: $\\frac{1}{z} = \\frac{a - ib}{a^2+b^2}$.\n(ii) Correct: $\\left|\\frac{\\bar z}{|z|^2}\\right| = \\frac{|z|}{|z|^2} = \\frac{1}{|z|}$.\n(iii) Correct: this is an NCERT property of the conjugate.\n(iv) Incorrect: $\\frac{(1+i)^2}{2} = \\frac{2i}{2} = i$, not $-i$.\n(v) Correct: $|i| = 1$, so $i^{-1} = \\bar{i} = -i$.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-133","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":133,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Writing $\\sqrt{-4} \\times \\sqrt{-9} = \\sqrt{(-4)(-9)} = 6$ is incorrect.\nReason (R): The rule $\\sqrt{a} \\times \\sqrt{b} = \\sqrt{ab}$ does not hold when both $a$ and $b$ are negative real numbers.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Correct working: $\\sqrt{-4}\\sqrt{-9} = (2i)(3i) = 6i^2 = -6$, not $6$. So A is true.\nR is true. NCERT shows that applying the rule to two negatives gives $i^2 = \\sqrt{(-1)(-1)} = 1$, which contradicts $i^2 = -1$.\nThe wrong value $6$ arises exactly from misusing this rule, so R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-134","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":134,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) $z_1z_2=z_2z_1$ for all complex numbers $z_1, z_2$.\n(ii) The multiplicative identity of complex numbers is $0+i1$.\n(iii) Every complex number has a multiplicative inverse.\n(iv) $z_1(z_2+z_3)=z_1z_2+z_1z_3$ for all complex $z_1, z_2, z_3$.\n(v) The product of two purely imaginary numbers is a real number.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: this is the commutative law of multiplication.\n(ii) Incorrect: the multiplicative identity is $1+i0$, because $z\\cdot1=z$.\n(iii) Incorrect: $0$ has no multiplicative inverse. Only non-zero complex numbers have one.\n(iv) Correct: this is the distributive law.\n(v) Correct: $(bi)(di)=bd\\,i^2=-bd$, which is real.\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-135","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":135,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In the Argand plane, the point representing $\\bar{z}$ is the mirror image of the point representing $z$ in the real axis.\nReason (R): If $z=x+iy$, then $z$ and $\\bar{z}$ correspond to the points $(x, y)$ and $(x, -y)$ respectively.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true: $\\bar{z}=x-iy$ corresponds to the point $(x,-y)$.\nThe points $(x,y)$ and $(x,-y)$ have the same x-coordinate and opposite y-coordinates. So they are reflections of each other in the x-axis, which is the real axis. Hence A is true.\nR directly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-136","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":136,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"The equation $x^2 + 1 = 0$ has no solution in the real number system. Which statement gives the correct reason?","image":"","options":["The square of every real number is non-negative, so $x^2 = -1$ is impossible for real $x$","The discriminant of $x^2 + 1 = 0$ is zero","Every quadratic equation has exactly one real root","$-1$ is not a rational number"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"From $x^2 + 1 = 0$ we get $x^2 = -1$.\nFor every real $x$, $x^2 \\geq 0$, so no real number can satisfy $x^2 = -1$.\nOption (2) fails because the discriminant is $0 - 4 = -4$, which is not zero.\nOption (4) is false, since $-1$ is rational.\nThis is why we define $i = \\sqrt{-1}$ and extend the real numbers to the complex numbers.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-137","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":137,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"A complex number $z$ satisfies $z+2\\bar z = 6-4i$. The value of $|z|$ is","image":"","options":["$2\\sqrt5$","$2\\sqrt3$","6","$\\sqrt{10}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Let $z = x+iy$, so $\\bar z = x-iy$.\nThen $z+2\\bar z = (x+2x) + i(y-2y) = 3x - iy$.\nEquating real and imaginary parts with $6-4i$: $3x = 6$ gives $x = 2$, and $-y = -4$ gives $y = 4$.\nSo $|z| = \\sqrt{4+16} = \\sqrt{20} = 2\\sqrt5$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-138","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":138,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"If $(2x - 1) + i(y + 3) = 5 + 7i$, where $x$ and $y$ are real numbers, then the value of $x + y$ is:","image":"","options":["7","5","10","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $(2x-1) + i(y+3) = 5 + 7i$ with $x, y$ real.\nRule: two complex numbers are equal only when their real parts are equal and their imaginary parts are equal.\nReal parts: $2x - 1 = 5$, so $x = 3$.\nImaginary parts: $y + 3 = 7$, so $y = 4$.\nSo $x + y = 3 + 4 = 7$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-139","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":139,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is NOT true for all complex numbers $z_1$ and $z_2$?","image":"","options":["$\\overline{z_1 z_2} = \\bar{z_1}\\,\\bar{z_2}$","$|z_1 z_2| = |z_1|\\,|z_2|$","$|z_1 + z_2| = |z_1| + |z_2|$","$z_1\\bar{z_1} = |z_1|^2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) This is true; it is a standard property of the conjugate.\n(2) This is true; the modulus of a product equals the product of the moduli.\n(3) This is false in general. Take $z_1 = 1$ and $z_2 = i$: then $|1+i| = \\sqrt{2}$, but $|1| + |i| = 2$.\n(4) This is true, because $(a+ib)(a-ib) = a^2 + b^2$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-140","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":140,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): If $(1+i)(2-i)(3+i) = A + iB$, then $A^2 + B^2 = 100$.\nReason (R): For complex numbers $z_1$ and $z_2$, $|z_1 z_2| = |z_1|\\,|z_2|$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Checking R: $|z_1z_2| = |z_1||z_2|$ is a standard property, so R is true.\nChecking A using R: $A^2+B^2 = |A+iB|^2 = |1+i|^2\\,|2-i|^2\\,|3+i|^2 = 2 \\times 5 \\times 10 = 100$.\nDirect check: $(1+i)(2-i) = 3+i$, and $(3+i)^2 = 8+6i$, giving $64+36 = 100$. So A is true.\nThe product rule for moduli is exactly what gives A, so R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-141","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":141,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"In the Argand plane, the complex number $z = -2+5i$ is represented by the point P. The point representing $\\bar{z}$ is","image":"","options":["$(2, 5)$","$(-2, -5)$","$(2, -5)$","$(5, -2)$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Conjugation keeps the real part and changes the sign of the imaginary part: $\\bar{z} = -2-5i$.\nThis corresponds to the point $(-2, -5)$.\nGeometrically, $\\bar{z}$ is the mirror image of $z$ in the real axis (x-axis).\nOption (1) is the reflection in the imaginary axis, which gives $-\\bar{z}$, not $\\bar{z}$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-142","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":142,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"For a real number $k$, the complex number $z = (k^2 - 4) + i(k + 2)$ is purely imaginary and non-zero. The value of $k$ is","image":"","options":["$-2$","$2$","$4$","$0$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A purely imaginary non-zero number needs $\\text{Re } z = 0$ and $\\text{Im } z \\neq 0$. \nFrom $\\text{Re } z = 0$: $k^2 - 4 = 0$, so $k = 2$ or $k = -2$. \nCheck $k = -2$: $\\text{Im } z = -2 + 2 = 0$, so $z = 0 + i0$, which is the zero complex number. Reject it. \nCheck $k = 2$: $\\text{Im } z = 4 \\neq 0$, so $z = 4i$, which is purely imaginary. \n$k = 4$ gives $z = 12 + 6i$ and $k = 0$ gives $z = -4 + 2i$; neither has zero real part. \nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-143","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":143,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"The real part of $\\frac{2 + i}{2 - i}$ is","image":"","options":["$\\frac{4}{5}$","$1$","$\\frac{3}{5}$","$\\frac{1}{5}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Multiply the numerator and the denominator by $2 + i$.\nNumerator: $(2 + i)^2 = 4 + 4i + i^2 = 3 + 4i$.\nDenominator: $(2 - i)(2 + i) = 4 + 1 = 5$.\nSo the quotient is $\\frac{3}{5} + \\frac{4}{5}i$, and its real part is $\\frac{3}{5}$.\nOption (1), $\\frac{4}{5}$, is the imaginary part, not the real part.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-144","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":144,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Express $(3 - 5i) + (-7 + 2i)$ in the form $a + ib$.","image":"","options":["$10 - 7i$","$-4 - 3i$","$-4 - 7i$","$4 + 3i$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Rule: $(a+ib)+(c+id) = (a+c)+i(b+d)$.\nReal part: $3 + (-7) = -4$.\nImaginary part: $-5 + 2 = -3$.\nSo the sum is $-4 - 3i$.\nThe option $-4 - 7i$ comes from wrongly subtracting the imaginary parts.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-145","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":145,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: For any complex numbers $z_1, z_2, z_3$, we have $z_1(z_2+z_3)=z_1z_2+z_1z_3$.\nStatement II: The proof of $(z_1+z_2)^2=z_1^2+2z_1z_2+z_2^2$ for complex numbers uses the commutative law of multiplication to combine $z_2z_1$ and $z_1z_2$.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is true. It is the distributive law, which complex multiplication obeys.\nStatement II is true. Expanding gives $(z_1+z_2)^2=z_1^2+z_2z_1+z_1z_2+z_2^2$.\nOnly the commutative law ($z_2z_1=z_1z_2$) lets the two middle terms combine into $2z_1z_2$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-146","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":146,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are true for ALL complex numbers $z_1, z_2$ (with $z_2\\neq0$ where needed)?\n(i) $|z_1z_2|=|z_1||z_2|$\n(ii) $\\overline{z_1+z_2}=\\bar z_1+\\bar z_2$\n(iii) $|z_1+z_2|=|z_1|+|z_2|$\n(iv) $\\text{Re}(z_1z_2)=\\text{Re}\\,z_1\\cdot\\text{Re}\\,z_2$\n(v) $\\left|\\frac{z_1}{z_2}\\right|=\\frac{|z_1|}{|z_2|}$\n(vi) $z_1\\bar z_1=|z_1|^2$","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i), (ii) and (v) are true; these are the NCERT modulus and conjugate properties.\n(iii) False: for $z_1=1$ and $z_2=i$, $|1+i|=\\sqrt2$, but $|1|+|i|=2$.\n(iv) False: with $z_1=a+ib$ and $z_2=c+id$, $\\text{Re}(z_1z_2)=ac-bd$, not $ac$. For example, $\\text{Re}(i\\cdot i)=-1$, while $\\text{Re}\\,i\\cdot\\text{Re}\\,i=0$.\n(vi) True: $(a+ib)(a-ib)=a^2+b^2$.\nFour statements are true for all $z_1, z_2$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-147","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":147,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"The square roots of $-25$, and the value represented by the symbol $\\sqrt{-25}$, are respectively","image":"","options":["$\\pm 5i$ and $5i$","$\\pm 5i$ and $\\pm 5i$","$5i$ only and $5i$","$\\pm 5$ and $-5$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"$(5i)^2=25i^2=-25$ and $(-5i)^2=25i^2=-25$, so both $5i$ and $-5i$ are square roots of $-25$.\nBy convention, the symbol $\\sqrt{-25}$ denotes only $\\sqrt{25}\\,i=5i$. This is just as $\\sqrt{-1}$ means $i$ only.\nOption (2) wrongly gives the symbol two values.\nOption (4) uses real numbers, whose squares can never be negative.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-148","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":148,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Which option correctly gives both square roots of $-25$, and the value meant by the symbol $\\sqrt{-25}$?","image":"","options":["Square roots: $\\pm 5i$; $\\sqrt{-25} = 5i$","Square roots: $\\pm 5i$; $\\sqrt{-25} = \\pm 5i$","Square roots: $\\pm 5$; $\\sqrt{-25} = 5i$","Square root: $5i$ only; $\\sqrt{-25} = 5i$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Check both values: $(5i)^2 = 25i^2 = -25$ and $(-5i)^2 = -25$. So both $5i$ and $-5i$ are square roots of $-25$.\nNCERT convention: for $a > 0$, the symbol $\\sqrt{-a}$ means only $\\sqrt{a}\\,i$. So $\\sqrt{-25} = 5i$.\nOption (2) is wrong because the symbol stands for one value, not two.\nOption (3) is wrong because $(\\pm 5)^2 = 25$, not $-25$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-149","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":149,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"If $x$ and $y$ are real numbers satisfying $(x + iy) + (3 - 2i) = (2y - ix) - (1 + i)$, then the value of $y - x$ is","image":"","options":["1","$-\\frac{7}{3}$","3","$\\frac{7}{3}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"LHS $= (x + 3) + i(y - 2)$.\nRHS $= (2y - 1) + i(-x - 1)$.\nEquate real parts: $x + 3 = 2y - 1$, so $x = 2y - 4$.\nEquate imaginary parts: $y - 2 = -x - 1$, so $y = 1 - x$.\nSubstitute: $x = 2(1 - x) - 4$, so $3x = -2$, giving $x = -\\frac{2}{3}$ and $y = \\frac{5}{3}$.\nSo $y - x = \\frac{7}{3}$. The tempting value 1 is $x + y$, not $y - x$.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-150","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":150,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Express the product $(4-3i)(2+5i)$ in the form $a+ib$.","image":"","options":["$23+14i$","$-7+14i$","$23-14i$","$8-15i$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Use $(a+ib)(c+id)=(ac-bd)+i(ad+bc)$ with $a=4,\\ b=-3,\\ c=2,\\ d=5$.\nReal part: $ac-bd=8-(-3)(5)=8+15=23$.\nImaginary part: $ad+bc=4(5)+(-3)(2)=20-6=14$.\nSo the product is $23+14i$.\nOption (2) comes from taking $8-15$, which forgets that $i^2=-1$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-151","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":151,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"For how many integers $n$ with $1\\le n\\le 100$ is $(1+i)^{2n}$ a negative real number?","image":"","options":["20","24","25","50"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"First, $(1+i)^2=1+2i+i^2=2i$.\nSo $(1+i)^{2n}=(2i)^n=2^n\\,i^n$.\nSince $2^n>0$, this is negative real only when $i^n=-1$, that is, when $n=4k+2$.\nThe values are $n=2,6,10,\\dots,98$.\nThe number of terms is $\\frac{98-2}{4}+1=25$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-152","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":152,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"The real part of $\\frac{3 + i}{1 - i}$ is","image":"","options":["2","1","-1","3/2"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Multiply numerator and denominator by the conjugate $1 + i$:\n$\\frac{(3+i)(1+i)}{(1-i)(1+i)} = \\frac{3 + 3i + i + i^2}{1 + 1}$.\nThis equals $\\frac{2 + 4i}{2} = 1 + 2i$.\nSo Re $= 1$. The value 2 is the imaginary part.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-153","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":153,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"For how many integers $n$ with $1 \\le n \\le 100$ is $(1+i)^{2n}$ a real number?","image":"","options":["25","50","75","100"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"First, $(1+i)^2 = 1 + 2i + i^2 = 2i$.\nSo $(1+i)^{2n} = (2i)^n = 2^n i^n$.\nSince $2^n$ is real and non-zero, the expression is real exactly when $i^n$ is real.\nNow $i^n$ equals $\\pm 1$ when $n$ is even and $\\pm i$ when $n$ is odd.\nThe even integers from 1 to 100 number 50.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-154","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":154,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): If $z$ is a complex number with $|z| = 1$, then $\\frac{1}{z} = \\bar z$.\nReason (R): For every complex number $z$, $z\\bar z = |z|^2$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true: $(a+ib)(a-ib) = a^2+b^2 = |z|^2$.\nA is true: if $|z| = 1$, then R gives $z\\bar z = 1$, so $\\bar z = \\frac1z$. Note that $z \\neq 0$ here, so $\\frac1z$ exists.\nA follows directly from R, so R is the correct explanation of A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-155","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":155,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"If $(x + iy)(2 - 3i) = 4 + i$, where $x$ and $y$ are real, then $x + y$ equals:","image":"","options":["$\\frac{19}{13}$","$1$","$\\frac{9}{13}$","$\\frac{11}{13}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Expand the left side: $(x+iy)(2-3i) = 2x - 3ix + 2iy - 3i^2 y = (2x + 3y) + i(2y - 3x)$.\nEquate parts: $2x + 3y = 4$ and $-3x + 2y = 1$.\nSolve: multiply the first by 3 and the second by 2, then add: $13y = 14$, so $y = \\frac{14}{13}$.\nThen $2x = 4 - \\frac{42}{13} = \\frac{10}{13}$, so $x = \\frac{5}{13}$.\nSo $x + y = \\frac{19}{13}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-156","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":156,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Let $z = x + iy$ be represented by the point P$(x, y)$ in the Argand plane, with $x \\ne 0$ and $y \\ne 0$.\nStatement I: The point representing $\\bar{z}$ is the mirror image of P in the real axis.\nStatement II: The point representing $-\\bar{z}$ is the mirror image of P in the imaginary axis.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"$\\bar{z} = x - iy$ corresponds to the point $(x, -y)$. This is the reflection of $(x, y)$ in the x-axis, which is the real axis. Statement I is true, as stated in NCERT.\n$-\\bar{z} = -x + iy$ corresponds to the point $(-x, y)$. This is the reflection of $(x, y)$ in the y-axis, which is the imaginary axis. Statement II is true.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-157","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":157,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): $\\sqrt{-4}\\times\\sqrt{-9} = -6$.\nReason (R): $\\sqrt{a}\\times\\sqrt{b} = \\sqrt{ab}$ holds for all real numbers $a$ and $b$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Check A: $\\sqrt{-4} = 2i$ and $\\sqrt{-9} = 3i$, so the product is $6i^2 = -6$. A is true.\nCheck R: if R held for negative numbers, we would get $\\sqrt{-4}\\sqrt{-9} = \\sqrt{36} = 6$, which contradicts A.\nNCERT shows that $\\sqrt{a}\\sqrt{b} \\neq \\sqrt{ab}$ when both $a$ and $b$ are negative. So R is false.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-158","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":158,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"If $z_1 = 3 + 2i$ and $z_2 = 1 - i$, then $\\frac{z_1 + z_2 + i}{z_1 - z_2 - 2}$ in the form $a + ib$ is","image":"","options":["$\\frac{2}{3} + \\frac{4}{3}i$","$-\\frac{2}{3} - \\frac{4}{3}i$","$\\frac{4}{3} - \\frac{2}{3}i$","$\\frac{2}{3} - \\frac{4}{3}i$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Numerator: $z_1 + z_2 + i = (4 + i) + i = 4 + 2i$.\nDenominator: $z_1 - z_2 - 2 = (2 + 3i) - 2 = 3i$.\nThen $\\frac{4+2i}{3i} \\times \\frac{-3i}{-3i} = \\frac{-12i - 6i^2}{9} = \\frac{6 - 12i}{9}$.\nSo the result is $\\frac{2}{3} - \\frac{4}{3}i$.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-159","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":159,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following quadratic equations has no real root and therefore needs complex numbers for its solution?","image":"","options":["$x^2 - 4x + 4 = 0$","$x^2 + 2x + 5 = 0$","$x^2 - x - 6 = 0$","$2x^2 + 5x + 2 = 0$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A quadratic $ax^2+bx+c=0$ has no real root when $D = b^2 - 4ac < 0$. \n(1) $D = 16 - 16 = 0$, so it has a real repeated root $x = 2$. \n(2) $D = 4 - 20 = -16 < 0$, so it has no real root. Its roots are $-1 \\pm 2i$. \n(3) $D = 1 + 24 = 25 > 0$, so it has the real roots 3 and $-2$. \n(4) $D = 25 - 16 = 9 > 0$, so it has the real roots $-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $-2$. \nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-160","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":160,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"If $x$ and $y$ are real numbers such that $(x+iy)(2-i)=7+4i$, then $x+y$ equals","image":"","options":["5","1","7","3"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Expand: $(x+iy)(2-i)=(2x+y)+i(2y-x)$.\nEquate real parts: $2x+y=7$.\nEquate imaginary parts: $-x+2y=4$.\nFrom the second equation, $x=2y-4$. Substituting into the first: $4y-8+y=7$, so $y=3$ and $x=2$.\nSo $x+y=5$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-161","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":161,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"If $(x - iy)^2 = \\frac{3-4i}{1-i}$, where $x$ and $y$ are real, then $x^2 + y^2$ equals","image":"","options":["$\\frac{25}{2}$","$\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}}$","$\\frac{5}{2}$","$\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{2}}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $(x-iy)^2 = \\frac{3-4i}{1-i}$.\nTake modulus of both sides: $|x-iy|^2 = \\frac{|3-4i|}{|1-i|} = \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}}$.\nSince $|x-iy|^2 = x^2+y^2$, we get $x^2+y^2 = \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}}$.\nThe value $\\frac{25}{2}$ is $(x^2+y^2)^2$, not $x^2+y^2$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-162","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":162,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"If $z_1=2+3i$ and $z_2=5-i$, then $\\text{Re}(z_1z_2)$ equals","image":"","options":["13","7","10","-3"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Formula: $\\text{Re}(z_1z_2)=\\text{Re}\\,z_1\\,\\text{Re}\\,z_2-\\text{Im}\\,z_1\\,\\text{Im}\\,z_2$.\nSubstituting: $2\\times5-(3)(-1)=10+3=13$.\nCheck: $z_1z_2=10-2i+15i-3i^2=13+13i$, whose real part is 13.\nOption (3) comes from multiplying only the real parts.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-163","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":163,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"In the Argand plane, a point A represents $z = -3+5i$. Which point represents $\\bar z$?","image":"","options":["$(-3, -5)$","$(3, 5)$","$(3, -5)$","$(5, -3)$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The conjugate of $z = -3+5i$ is $\\bar z = -3-5i$.\nThe complex number $x+iy$ corresponds to the point $(x, y)$, so $\\bar z$ corresponds to $(-3, -5)$.\nGeometrically, $\\bar z$ is the mirror image of $z$ in the real axis: x stays the same and y changes sign.\nThe point $(3,-5)$ is the reflection in the origin, which represents $-z$, not $\\bar z$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-164","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":164,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are equal to 1?\n$i^{100}$, $i^{-4}$, $i^{22}$, $i^{-35}$, $(-i)^{8}$","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Use $i^{4k} = 1$, $i^{4k+1} = i$, $i^{4k+2} = -1$, $i^{4k+3} = -i$.\n$i^{100} = i^{4 \\cdot 25} = 1$.\n$i^{-4} = 1$.\n$i^{22} = i^{4 \\cdot 5 + 2} = -1$.\n$i^{-35} = i^{-36+1} = i$.\n$(-i)^8 = (-1)^8 i^8 = 1$.\nThree of the five equal 1.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-165","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":165,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"For a real number $k$, the product $(2+ki)(3-2i)(1+i)$ is a real number. The value of this real number is","image":"","options":["$10.4$","$9.6$","$8$","$12$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Step 1: $(2+ki)(3-2i)=(6+2k)+i(3k-4)$.\nStep 2: Multiply by $(1+i)$.\nReal part: $(6+2k)-(3k-4)=10-k$.\nImaginary part: $(6+2k)+(3k-4)=2+5k$.\nStep 3: For a real product, $2+5k=0$, so $k=-\\frac{2}{5}$.\nStep 4: The product is $10-k=10+0.4=10.4$.\nOption (2), $9.6$, comes from taking $k=+\\frac{2}{5}$ by mistake.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-166","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":166,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"Let $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ be complex numbers with $\\alpha \\ne \\beta$ and $\\bar{\\alpha}\\beta \\ne 1$.\nStatement I: If $|\\beta| = 1$, then $\\left|\\frac{\\beta - \\alpha}{1 - \\bar{\\alpha}\\beta}\\right| = 1$.\nStatement II: If $|\\alpha| = |\\beta| = 2$, then $\\left|\\frac{\\beta - \\alpha}{1 - \\bar{\\alpha}\\beta}\\right| = 1$.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I: $|\\beta|=1$ gives $\\beta\\bar{\\beta} = 1$. Then $|1-\\bar{\\alpha}\\beta| = |\\beta\\bar{\\beta} - \\bar{\\alpha}\\beta| = |\\beta|\\,|\\bar{\\beta}-\\bar{\\alpha}| = |\\overline{\\beta-\\alpha}| = |\\beta-\\alpha|$. So the ratio is 1. True.\nStatement II: take $\\alpha = 2$, $\\beta = -2$. Then $|\\beta-\\alpha| = 4$, while $|1-\\bar{\\alpha}\\beta| = |1+4| = 5$. The ratio is $\\frac{4}{5} \\ne 1$. False.\nThe result needs $|\\beta| = 1$ (or $|\\alpha| = 1$), not merely equal moduli.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-167","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":167,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"In the Argand plane, the point P$(-3, 4)$ represents a complex number $z$. Which of the following correctly describes the point representing $\\bar{z}$?","image":"","options":["$(-3,-4)$, lying in the third quadrant at a distance 5 from the origin","$(3, 4)$, lying in the first quadrant at a distance 5 from the origin","$(3,-4)$, lying in the fourth quadrant at a distance 5 from the origin","$(-3,-4)$, lying in the third quadrant at a distance 7 from the origin"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Here $z = -3 + 4i$, so $\\bar{z} = -3 - 4i$, which is the point $(-3,-4)$.\nThis point is the mirror image of P in the real axis, and it lies in the third quadrant.\nIts distance from the origin is $|\\bar{z}| = \\sqrt{9+16} = 5$.\nOption (3) reflects P in the origin instead, and option (4) wrongly adds $3+4$ to get the distance.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-COMPLEX-168","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","qno":168,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","type":"mcq","question":"The value of $i^{-97}$ is","image":"","options":["$i$","$-i$","$1$","$-1$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Write $i^{-97} = \\frac{1}{i^{97}}$.\nSince $97 = 4(24) + 1$, $i^{97} = i$.\nSo $i^{-97} = \\frac{1}{i} = \\frac{1}{i}\\times\\frac{i}{i} = \\frac{i}{-1} = -i$.\nCheck with the cyclic rule: $-97 = 4(-25) + 3$, so $i^{-97} = i^3 = -i$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 4 · Complex Numbers and Quadratic Equations","difficulty":"easy"}]});
